00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:31.700
And now for my discussion
with Dr. Justin Sonnenburg.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.059
Justin, thanks so much for being here.

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:35.200
- Great to be here.
- Yeah.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.830
I am a true novice when it
comes to the microbiome,

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:43.005
so I'd like to start off
with a really basic question,

00:08:43.005 --> 00:08:45.980
which is, what is the microbiome?

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:50.250
I imagine lots of little
bugs running around in my gut

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.980
and I don't quite like the image of that,

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:57.040
but I'm aware that our
microbiome can be good for us,

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:59.830
but we can also have an
unhealthy microbiome.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:03.350
So if I were to look at
the microbiome at the scale

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:05.000
that I could see the meaningful things,

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.100
what would it look like, and
what's going on in there?

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:11.740
- Yeah, I mean essentially you're correct.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.090
I mean, we have all of
these little microorganisms

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:15.170
running around in our gut.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.603
I think, just to start off
with clarifying terminology,

00:09:20.124 --> 00:09:23.675
microbiome and microbiota
quite often are referred to,

00:09:23.675 --> 00:09:27.130
or used to refer to our microbial
community interchangeably,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.910
and I'll probably switch
between those two terms today.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:32.350
The other important thing to
realize is that these microbes

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:36.390
are not just in our gut, but
they're all over our body.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:38.710
They're in our nose,
they're in our mouths,

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:40.050
they're on our skin.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:44.090
And so basically anywhere that
the environment can get to

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:47.310
in our body, which includes
inside our digestive tract,

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:50.050
of course, is colonized with microbes.

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:53.325
And the vast majority of these

00:09:53.325 --> 00:09:56.300
are in our distal gut and in our colon,

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:59.570
and so this is the gut
microbiota or gut microbiome.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:04.380
And the density of this
community is astounding.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:05.693
I mean, it really is.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.850
If you get down to the
scale of being able to see

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:15.830
individual microbes, you start
off with a zoomed out view

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:18.410
and you see something that
looks like fecal material

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:22.450
that digest inside the
gut, and you zoom in,

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.880
and you start to get to
the microscopic level

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:25.964
and see the microbes.

00:10:25.964 --> 00:10:29.380
They are just packed
side to side, end to end.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:32.120
It's a super dense bacterial community,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.247
almost like a biofilm,

00:10:34.247 --> 00:10:36.660
something that's just made up of microbes,

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:40.280
to the point where it's
thought that around 30%

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.890
of fecal matter is microbes, 30 to 50%.

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:47.990
So it's an incredibly
dense microbial community,

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:52.520
we're talking of trillions
of microbial cells.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:54.610
And all those microbial cells,

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:58.804
if you start to get to know
them, and see who they are,

00:10:58.804 --> 00:11:02.140
break out in the gut probably to hundreds,

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:04.870
to 1,000 species, depending upon

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:08.020
how you define microbial species.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:11.068
And then most of these are bacteria,

00:11:11.068 --> 00:11:14.060
but there are a lot of
other life forms there.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:17.610
There are archaea, which
are little microbes

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:20.980
that are bacterial-like,
but they're different.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.260
There are eukaryotes.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:27.200
So we commonly think of
eukaryotes in the gut

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.980
as something like a parasite.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:33.230
But there are eukaryotes,

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:36.870
there are fungi, there
are also little viruses.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:38.790
There are these bacteriophages

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:41.203
that infect bacterial cells.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:46.010
And those actually outnumber
the bacteria, like 10 to one.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:50.370
So they're just everywhere
there, they kill bacteria.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.170
And so there's these really interesting

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:54.000
predator/prey interactions.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.960
But overall, it's just this really dense,

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.900
complex, dynamic ecosystem.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:04.900
And so, we're talking about
the human as a single species,

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:07.550
but we're also thinking of
the human as this complex,

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:11.680
integrated ecosystem of hundreds
to thousands of species,

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.020
interacting concert to do
all the fantastic things

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:17.110
that we know happen in the human body.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:19.083
- Amazing. So we've got a lot of cargo.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:21.160
Or maybe we're the cargo.
- Yeah.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:22.820
- Who knows?
- Yeah, I mean,

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.530
there have been people
that have likened humans

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:28.940
to just a really elaborate
culturing flask for microbes,

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:32.090
and that we've actually been designed

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:33.676
over the course of evolution,

00:12:33.676 --> 00:12:38.640
designed to just efficiently propagate

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:41.350
this microbial culture
from person to person,

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.030
from generation to generation.

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:45.180
So it's a different way of
thinking of the human body.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:46.013
- Interesting.

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:52.390
I believe that our pH or the
pH of our digestive system

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:54.940
varies as you descend,

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:58.500
as you go from mouth
to throat and stomach.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.860
And you said that most of the microbiota

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:02.763
are in the distal colon.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.800
Are there distinct forms
of microbiota all along

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.790
the length of the digestive tract,

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:12.670
and within these other interfaces
with the outside world?

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:13.503
- Totally. Yeah.

00:13:13.503 --> 00:13:15.860
So it starts with our
teeth and in our mouth,

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:19.630
and saliva, there's a oral microbiota.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:22.310
These microbial species are very different

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:24.670
than the ones that you find
in the digestive tract.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:29.670
They're usually built to
deal with oxygen very well.

00:13:30.243 --> 00:13:33.650
They're in an area that is
exposed to a lot of oxygen,

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.190
they of course see different
nutrients than, for instance,

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:38.110
the colonic bacteria would see.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:42.710
And they grow quite often
in mats that live on teeth.

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.970
So they're very structured in terms of...

00:13:47.305 --> 00:13:49.870
And not moving around a lot.

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:52.220
So they're very, fairly stationary.

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:53.830
As you move down the digestive tract,

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:56.230
there are microbes in
esophagus in our stomach,

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:59.600
but those communities are not very dense,

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:01.190
and actually not very well studied.

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:03.460
We know of a very...

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:05.440
There's a very famous stomach bacteria

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:07.142
known as Helicobacter pylori,

00:14:07.142 --> 00:14:10.940
which can cause stomach ulcers
and cause gastric cancer

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:15.430
in some less frequent situations.

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:17.540
But this is

00:14:19.195 --> 00:14:23.730
a very different set of
microbes, they have to be adapted

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:25.690
to a different environment,
in the stomach especially,

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:27.910
incredibly acidic environment.

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:29.250
But also very different in terms

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:32.130
of their ability to interact
with other microbes,

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:34.060
just because the
communities are less dense,

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:37.860
they're less dynamic, there's
less nutrients that stay there

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.000
and passage through the community.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.170
So a lot of times those communities

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:45.180
are reliant upon nutrients
derived from the host,

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.490
as opposed to nutrients
derived from our diet.

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:49.020
As you move down out of the stomach,

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:50.170
into the small intestine,

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:52.210
you start to see these communities,

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:55.355
which are the ones that are
becoming more well-studied.

00:14:55.355 --> 00:14:58.180
Small intestine is still
a bit of a black box,

00:14:58.180 --> 00:14:59.770
just because it's hard to access.

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:01.940
And so there's some really
cool technologies out there

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:05.597
for using, for instance,
capsules to do sampling

00:15:05.597 --> 00:15:09.070
as the capsule passes
through the digestive tract,

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:10.820
so that we have a better
idea of what's going on

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:12.080
in the small intestine.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:15.440
And then you get to the colon,
and this is the community

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:20.270
that's just so incredibly
densely packed, doing a ton of...

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.995
There's a ton of metabolic
activity happening there,

00:15:22.995 --> 00:15:26.350
and a bunch of interaction with the host.

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:31.350
And that's the community
that's really the best studied.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.560
Part of the reason for
that is because stool

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.060
is so easy to obtain
compared to, for instance,

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:39.170
something in the stomach
or small intestine.

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:42.500
And that stool is fairly
representative, we know,

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:43.870
from studies that have been done

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:45.640
using colonoscopies and so forth.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:47.240
Stool is fairly representative

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:49.120
of what's happening in the colon.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:51.690
So dense, super exciting community,

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:52.860
but also the best studied,

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.347
just because it's the easiest to access

00:15:55.347 --> 00:15:57.380
in the lower digestive tract.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:58.393
- Very interesting.

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:02.290
I imagine these microbiota
have to get in there

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:05.948
at some point, are
microbiota seen in newborns?

00:16:05.948 --> 00:16:08.500
In other words, where do they come from?

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:12.160
And dare I ask, what direction
do they enter the body?

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:14.790
Or is it from multiple directions?

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:16.290
- Yeah, yeah. Great question.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.870
So one of the burning questions
that we can come back to

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.120
at the end of this, is where
does our microbiota come from?

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:23.490
Because it is this kind of

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.220
existential question in the field,

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:28.420
like where is this
community assembling from?

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:30.970
And the reason that it's
such an interesting question

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:35.460
is that, a fetus, when it's in the womb,

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:37.840
that's actually a sterile environment.

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:39.530
There have been some
studies that have looked

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:41.910
at whether there are microbes in the womb,

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:45.165
and microbes colonizing
the fetus at that point.

00:16:45.165 --> 00:16:46.680
There's some debate about this,

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.780
but overall it looks like that's not a big

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:51.470
part of the equation of
microbial colonization.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:54.930
And so each time I'm an infant is born,

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:56.800
it's this new ecosystem.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.900
It's like an island
rising up out of the ocean

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:00.260
that has no species on it,

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:01.778
and suddenly there's this land rush

00:17:01.778 --> 00:17:04.930
for this open territory.

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:09.530
And so we know that infants go through

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:13.370
this really complex process
of microbiota assembly

00:17:13.370 --> 00:17:16.470
over the first days, weeks,
months, years of life.

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:20.760
And then you get into
switching to solid food,

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:22.880
two to three years of age.

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:26.170
There are some changes in
childhood, adolescence,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:27.590
working into adulthood.

00:17:27.590 --> 00:17:32.580
But that first zero to one year

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:36.530
is a super dynamic time
with really kind of

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:38.490
stereotypical developmental changes

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:42.250
in the gut microbiota, that
appear to have the possibility

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:46.540
of going wrong and causing
problems for infants

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:48.020
in some instances.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:51.730
But if you step away
from that extreme side

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:53.150
of things going wrong,

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:56.240
there also were a lot of
different trajectories

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:57.870
that developmental process can take,

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:01.980
because our microbiota is
so malleable and so plastic,

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:03.840
and those trajectories can be affected

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.400
by all sorts of factors in early life.

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:09.170
So an example is whether an infant is born

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:11.920
by C-section or born vaginally.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.030
We know from beautiful work
that's been done in the field

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:18.260
that infants that are born by C-section

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:22.570
actually have a gut microbiota that looks

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:26.000
more like human skin than it does

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:30.690
like either the birth canal,
the vagina microbiota,

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:35.280
or the mother's stool microbiota.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.220
Babies that are born
through the birth canal

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:40.980
have initial colonization
of vaginal microbes

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:43.220
and of stool microbes from their mother.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:44.850
And so just these first days,

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:46.200
whether you're born by C-section

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:48.530
or through natural childbirth,

00:18:48.530 --> 00:18:51.090
your gut microbiota looks very different.

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.100
And then compound on top of that,

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:55.470
whether you're breast fed or formula fed,

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:58.490
whether your family has a
pet or doesn't have a pet,

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:00.617
whether you're exposed to antibiotics,

00:19:00.617 --> 00:19:03.170
there are all these factors that really

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.750
can change that developmental process,

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:07.820
and really change your microbial identity,

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:09.360
eventually in life.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:14.360
The reason that the field is
paying really close attention

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:16.250
to this and studying this right now

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:20.486
is because we know from animal studies,

00:19:20.486 --> 00:19:25.486
that depending upon the microbes
that you get early in life,

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:30.240
you can send the immune
system or metabolism

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.652
of an organism or other
parts of their biology

00:19:32.652 --> 00:19:35.580
in totally different
developmental trajectories.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:39.140
So what microbes you're
colonized with early in life

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:40.327
can really change your biology,

00:19:40.327 --> 00:19:41.970
and we can come back to that later, but-

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:43.830
- Yeah, we should.

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:45.260
- Getting back to that original question

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:47.420
of where do your microbes come from,

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:49.300
you'd think because you're born

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:50.840
through your mother's birth canal,

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:52.570
or exposed to her skin microbes,

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:55.750
that a lot of your microbes
would come from your mother,

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:57.285
but it actually turns out that,

00:19:57.285 --> 00:19:59.450
we can certainly detect that signal,

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:02.730
we certainly see maternal
microbes in the infant,

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:04.830
but there are a lot of
microbes that are coming

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:08.640
from other places, surfaces, other people,

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:12.250
perhaps other caregivers, but
perhaps strangers as well.

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:16.224
So we acquire our microbes
from a variety of sources.

00:20:16.224 --> 00:20:18.510
The first ones are from our mom,

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.640
or from our caregivers from the hospital,

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:22.790
but then we add to that tremendously

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:24.680
over the first year or so of life.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:27.060
- Incredible, you even said pets.

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:32.060
So if there's a dog in the
home or a parakeet in the home,

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:35.080
clearly they have a microbiome also,

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:38.280
and potentially the child
is deriving microbiota

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:40.670
species from those pets, correct?

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:41.503
- Exactly, yeah.

00:20:41.503 --> 00:20:46.140
And so the best studies
that have been done

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:48.880
have just looked at pets in
the household as a factor,

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:52.840
and whether that changes
the group of infants

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:55.000
that have a pet to look slightly different

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:57.510
than the group of infants
that don't have a pet.

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:58.610
And then the question is,

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:01.270
what is the pet doing to
change those microbes?

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:03.920
And some of it is probably
actually contributing,

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:05.503
direct members of the microbiota.

00:21:05.503 --> 00:21:10.390
Actually, I have a dog,
that dog occasionally

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:13.580
will lick my mouth without
me paying attention,

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:15.675
and that's probably introducing microbes.

00:21:15.675 --> 00:21:20.190
We also know that pets
are down in the dirt,

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:23.750
they're outside, they're being exposed

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:25.720
to a lot of environmental microbes.

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:30.120
And so just pets serving as a conduit

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:33.170
for a bunch of microbes
that we wouldn't otherwise

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:36.310
come in contact with is
a possibility as well.

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:39.862
- Well, we will return to pets,
and in particular your dog,

00:21:39.862 --> 00:21:41.310
an amazing dog, by the way.

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:43.250
I met your dog just the other day,

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:44.480
and I had to force myself,

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:46.227
I had to pry myself away from...

00:21:46.227 --> 00:21:47.730
It's a Havanese, right?
- Havanese.

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:48.968
- [Andrew Huberman] Incredible.
What is your dog's name?

00:21:48.968 --> 00:21:50.640
- Louis. Louis Pasteur.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:51.860
- Louis Pasteur.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:53.390
- How appropriate.

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:55.150
Amazing dog, what a personality

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:56.433
on that dog.
- Yeah.

00:21:57.836 --> 00:21:59.514
- The issue that I think

00:21:59.514 --> 00:22:02.760
a lot of people are probably wondering is,

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:05.015
what is a healthy microbiome?

00:22:05.015 --> 00:22:07.050
What is it supporting?

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:09.960
We hear that you need a healthy microbiome

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:11.900
to support the immune
system or metabolism,

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:14.010
or even the gut-brain axis.

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:17.720
How do we define a healthy,
versus a unhealthy microbiome?

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:19.850
Some people might know
the unhealthy microbiome

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:23.660
is dysbiosis is the word that
I encounter in the literature.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:26.213
But given that there's so
many species of microbiota,

00:22:26.213 --> 00:22:29.400
and given that I think we
probably each have a signature

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:30.854
pattern of microbiota,

00:22:30.854 --> 00:22:34.040
how do we define healthy
versus unhealthy microbiota?

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:35.420
Is there a test for this?

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:38.697
Later we'll talk about technologies
for testing microbiota.

00:22:38.697 --> 00:22:41.050
There are a lot of companies now,

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:44.380
a lot of people sending
stool samples in the mail.

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.180
Never look at the Postal
Service the same way again,

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:49.380
but it's out there and
it's getting analyzed.

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:50.810
So how should I think about this?

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:52.890
I can think about things like heart rate,

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:57.890
heart rate variability, BMI,
all sorts of metrics of health.

00:22:57.960 --> 00:22:59.750
How should I think about the microbiota?

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:02.100
How do I know if my microbiome
is healthy or unhealthy?

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:04.750
- Yeah, it's a million
dollar question right now

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:06.641
in the field, and there's
a lot of different ways

00:23:06.641 --> 00:23:08.340
of thinking about that, and I
can talk about some of those.

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:13.340
But I would say that there
are sessions at conferences,

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:15.280
there are review articles
being commissioned.

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:18.280
There are all sorts of thought
pieces about this right now,

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:19.950
like what is a healthy microbiota?

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:22.260
What are the features that define it?

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:25.010
And I think before diving into this,

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:30.010
the important thing to realize
is, it's a complex topic.

00:23:31.227 --> 00:23:33.280
Context matters a lot.

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:35.450
What's healthy for one
person or one population

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:38.760
may not be healthy for
another person or population.

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:43.570
And the microbiota is malleable.

00:23:43.570 --> 00:23:48.570
It's plastic, it changes
our human biology,

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:52.720
which I think is, how we think
about health quite often,

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:56.370
BMI and longevity, reproductive success,

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:57.770
however you want to define it.

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:03.810
It certainly can accommodate a variety

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:08.030
of configurations of gut
microbiota, and we don't have...

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:12.000
It's really hard to untangle
all of the different factors

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:14.030
of what could be very healthy,

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:16.092
versus a little bit less healthy.

00:24:16.092 --> 00:24:18.760
So I will say that there's
no single answer to this,

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:20.920
but there's some really
important considerations.

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:23.660
And perhaps the best way
to start talking about this

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:27.200
is to go back to the inception

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:28.680
of the Human Microbiome Project,

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:31.420
which was this program that NIH started.

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:34.604
They invested a lot of money in 2008, 2009

00:24:34.604 --> 00:24:39.604
for really propelling the field
of gut microbiome research.

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:43.810
It was becoming evident at that point

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:46.370
that this was not just a
curiosity of human biology,

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:48.610
that it was probably really
important for our health.

00:24:48.610 --> 00:24:51.640
And they had all this
wonderful sequencing technology

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:54.340
from the Human Genome Sequencing Project,

00:24:54.340 --> 00:24:56.640
and with the human genome
completed that point,

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.170
they started turning that technology

00:24:58.170 --> 00:24:59.610
to sequencing our gut microbes.

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:01.890
And it's important to contextualize

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:05.520
the amount of information that
they're trying to document,

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:09.890
the collective genome of our
gut microbes is on the order

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:14.890
of 100 to 500 times larger
than our human genome.

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:17.440
So it's just in terms
of the number of genes.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:20.030
So it's just this vast number of genes,

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:21.420
and then if you start getting into

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:24.940
some of the fine variation,
it's scales by 10 to a 100 fold.

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:27.200
So really a huge amount information

00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:28.180
they're trying to document.

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:31.930
And so it was a wonderful investment,

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:34.544
and it continues to pay
dividends to this day.

00:25:34.544 --> 00:25:37.780
But one of their goals of that
project was to try to define

00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:41.230
what a healthy microbiome is,
versus a diseased microbiome

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:42.190
in different contexts.

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:45.720
And so they started enrolling
a bunch of healthy people

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:47.090
and a bunch of people with, for instance,

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:49.470
inflammatory bowel disease
and other diseases.

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:52.725
And the idea was, let's
document those microbiomes.

00:25:52.725 --> 00:25:55.410
What microbes are there,
what genes are there?

00:25:55.410 --> 00:25:57.050
And then we can start to get a sense of,

00:25:57.050 --> 00:26:00.000
what are the commonalities
of the healthy people,

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:04.693
and how can that go wrong in
these different disease states?

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:10.560
There were some answers from that,

00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:13.470
but through those studies,

00:26:13.470 --> 00:26:14.950
we really started to get the image

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:17.590
that there is this
tremendous individuality

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:18.970
in the gut microbiome.

00:26:18.970 --> 00:26:23.970
And so it's really hard to
start drawing conclusions

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:28.440
after initial pass of that project,

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.590
of what is a healthy microbiome?

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:32.700
But the other thing that
we started to realize

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:35.325
at the same time, there
were studies going on

00:26:35.325 --> 00:26:39.430
documenting the gut microbiome
of traditional populations

00:26:39.430 --> 00:26:43.680
of humans, hunter-gatherers,
rural agricultural populations.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:46.890
And those studies were really mind-blowing

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:49.930
from the perspective of all
these people are healthy,

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:52.450
they're living very different lifestyles,

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:54.650
and their microbiome doesn't look anything

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:57.570
like a healthy American microbiome.

00:26:57.570 --> 00:27:00.810
- So does that mean that the
healthy American microbiome

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:05.550
is healthy, but only in
the context of living

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:09.050
in the United States and
consuming what's consumed here?

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:12.095
Or is it that there is a
superior microbiome signature,

00:27:12.095 --> 00:27:14.840
somewhere in our history
or currently in the world?

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:19.530
- Yeah, I think that's kind
of a big question right now.

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:24.057
I think there's a great quote
from Dobzhansky that says,

00:27:24.057 --> 00:27:25.660
"Nothing in biology makes sense,

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:27.530
except in the light of evolution."

00:27:27.530 --> 00:27:31.916
And these traditional
populations are all modern people

00:27:31.916 --> 00:27:33.990
living on the planet now,

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:38.990
but their lifestyle does represent
the closest approximation

00:27:39.370 --> 00:27:43.340
to how our ancestors, early humans lived.

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:46.070
And so those microbiomes,

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.920
and now we know from
sequencing of paleofeces,

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:51.140
the microbiome of these
traditional populations

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:55.200
is more representative of the microbiome

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:58.110
that we evolved with,
that potentially shaped

00:27:58.110 --> 00:27:59.430
our human genome.

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:04.430
And so one possibility is
that in industrialized world,

00:28:05.330 --> 00:28:08.417
we have a different microbiome
from traditional populations,

00:28:08.417 --> 00:28:10.500
and that microbiome is well-adapted

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:13.050
to our current lifestyle,
and therefore healthy

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:15.050
in the context of an
industrialized society.

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:17.720
And there probably are
elements of that that are true.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:20.740
But another possibility is
that this is a microbiome

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:21.947
that's gone off the rails,

00:28:21.947 --> 00:28:26.750
that it is deteriorating in
the face of antibiotic use,

00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:30.550
and all the problems associated

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:34.010
with an industrialized diet, Western diet.

00:28:34.010 --> 00:28:39.010
And that even though the
Human Microbiome Project

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:42.560
documented the microbiome
of healthy people,

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:44.870
healthy Americans, that what they really

00:28:44.870 --> 00:28:48.380
may have been documenting
there is a perturbed microbiota

00:28:48.380 --> 00:28:51.750
that's really predisposing
people to a variety

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:54.900
of inflammatory and metabolic diseases.

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:57.779
- It reminds me, as a neurobiologist,

00:28:57.779 --> 00:29:01.450
was weaned in the landscape
of so-called critical periods,

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:06.450
where early life environment
very strongly shapes the brain.

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:09.280
And so many studies were
done on animals raised

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:11.530
in traditional cages with a
water bottle and some food,

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:14.200
maybe a few other animals
of the same species.

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:16.200
And then people came along and said, wait,

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:18.520
normally these species in
the wild would have things

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:20.620
like things to climb over
and things to go through,

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:22.720
and you provide those very basic elements,

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:25.080
and all of a sudden the
architecture of neural circuits

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:26.280
looks very different, and you realize

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:28.773
that were studying a deprived condition.

00:29:28.773 --> 00:29:30.700
And earlier you actually referred to,

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:33.410
if I understood correctly,
to critical periods

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:35.513
for gut microbiome development.

00:29:36.430 --> 00:29:39.690
Is it fair to say that
there are critical periods?

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:41.300
Meaning, if let's say my...

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:42.133
Let's aim it at me.

00:29:42.133 --> 00:29:46.350
If my gut microbiome was dysbiotic,

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:50.260
it was off early in
life, can I rescue that

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:52.600
through proper conditions and exercise?

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:55.340
Or is there some sort of fixed pattern

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.220
that's going to be hard
for me to escape from?

00:29:57.220 --> 00:30:00.880
- Yeah, there's a big
field that's emerging now

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:04.290
that we refer to as kind of reprogramming

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:05.330
the gut microbiome.

00:30:05.330 --> 00:30:09.130
And I think if we want
to conceptualize humans

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:13.268
as this aggregate human microbial biology,

00:30:13.268 --> 00:30:15.827
most people have heard of CRISPR,

00:30:15.827 --> 00:30:19.130
and the ability to potentially
change our human genome

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.963
in ways that correct genetic problems.

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:28.100
That's a wonderful technology,
and has kind of put

00:30:28.100 --> 00:30:31.007
on the table genetic
engineering for cur disease,

00:30:31.007 --> 00:30:35.010
but it's much easier to change
gut microbes for a problem,

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:39.000
just because that community is malleable.

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:43.400
The issue that I think
we're seeing in the field

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:47.130
is that microbiomes quite often,

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:49.280
whether they're diseased or healthy,

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:50.900
exist in stable states.

00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:55.900
They kind of tend towards this
well that has gravity to it

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:58.040
in a way, biological gravity,

00:30:58.040 --> 00:31:00.780
where it's really hard to dislodge

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:02.700
that community from that state.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:06.520
So even individuals, for
instance, that get antibiotics.

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:09.130
You take oral antibiotics,

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:11.120
the community takes this huge hit.

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:13.059
We know that a bunch of microbes die,

00:31:13.059 --> 00:31:15.390
the composition changes.

00:31:15.390 --> 00:31:18.290
And that represents a
period of vulnerability

00:31:18.290 --> 00:31:20.980
where pathogens can come in and
take over and cause disease.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:24.680
But if that doesn't happen,
the microbiota kind of works

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:27.510
its way back to something
that is not exactly like,

00:31:27.510 --> 00:31:30.695
but similar to the
pre-antibiotic treatment.

00:31:30.695 --> 00:31:33.981
We know with dietary perturbations,

00:31:33.981 --> 00:31:37.040
quite often you'll see
a really rapid change

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:38.330
to the gut microbiome.

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:41.260
And then it's almost like a
memory where it snaps back

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:44.510
to something that's very
similar to the original state,

00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:46.900
even though the diet remains different.

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:48.820
And so there's this incredible,

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:51.191
what we refer to as resilience
of the gut microbiome,

00:31:51.191 --> 00:31:54.000
and resistance to change,

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:58.040
or at least resistance to
establishing a new stable state.

00:31:58.040 --> 00:31:59.700
So that doesn't mean it's hopeless

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:04.190
to change an unhealthy microbiome
to a healthy microbiome,

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:06.390
but it does mean that we
need to think carefully

00:32:06.390 --> 00:32:10.880
about restructuring
these communities in ways

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:14.067
where we can achieve a new
stable state that will resist

00:32:14.067 --> 00:32:16.830
the microbial community
getting pulled back

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:18.200
to that original state.

00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:22.814
And one of the really
simplest and nicest examples

00:32:22.814 --> 00:32:27.814
of this an experiment that
we performed with mice,

00:32:29.630 --> 00:32:32.698
where we were feeding
mice a normal mouse diet,

00:32:32.698 --> 00:32:35.510
a lot of nutrients there
for the gut microbiota,

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:37.210
things like dietary fiber.

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:40.740
And we switched those mice, half the mice,

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:42.380
to a low fiber diet.

00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:44.570
And we were basically asking the question

00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:47.750
that if you switch to kind
of a Western-like diet,

00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:49.930
a low fiber, higher fat diet,

00:32:49.930 --> 00:32:51.740
what happens to the gut microbiota?

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:54.930
And we saw the microbiota
change, it lost diversity.

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:58.660
It was very similar to what
we see in the difference

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:01.340
between industrialized and
traditional populations.

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:03.950
But when we brought back a healthy diet,

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:07.750
a lot of the microbes
returned, it was fairly...

00:33:07.750 --> 00:33:10.130
There was this kind of
memory where it went back

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:12.410
to very similar to its original state.

00:33:12.410 --> 00:33:14.960
The difference is that
when we put the mice

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:17.300
on the low fiber, high fat diet,

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:21.140
and then kept them on that
for multiple generations,

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:23.560
we saw this progressive deterioration

00:33:23.560 --> 00:33:25.200
over the course of generations

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:27.070
whereby the fourth generation,

00:33:27.070 --> 00:33:31.710
the gut microbiome was a fraction
of what it originally was.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:35.070
Let's say 30% of the species only remained

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:38.200
something like 70% of the
species had gone extinct,

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:40.420
or appeared to have gone extinct.

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:43.124
We then put those mice back
onto a high fiber diet,

00:33:43.124 --> 00:33:45.350
and we didn't see recovery.

00:33:45.350 --> 00:33:49.170
So in that case, it's a situation
where a new stable state

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:50.082
has been achieved.

00:33:50.082 --> 00:33:52.440
In that case, it's
probably because those mice

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:53.988
don't actually have access

00:33:53.988 --> 00:33:56.310
to the microbes that they've lost.

00:33:56.310 --> 00:33:59.570
And we actually know that we
did the control experiment

00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:02.730
of mice on a high fiber
diet for four generations.

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:04.280
They maintain all their microbes.

00:34:04.280 --> 00:34:06.740
If we take those fourth generation mice

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:09.540
with all the diversity
and do a fecal transplant

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:11.970
into the mice that had
lost their microbes,

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:13.834
but had been returned
to a high fiber diet,

00:34:13.834 --> 00:34:16.581
all of the diversity was reconstituted.

00:34:16.581 --> 00:34:19.530
So your question of like,

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:21.590
how do we establish new stable states?

00:34:21.590 --> 00:34:23.420
How do we get back to a healthy microbiota

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:25.550
if we have taken a lot of antibiotics,

00:34:25.550 --> 00:34:27.152
or have a deteriorated microbiota,

00:34:27.152 --> 00:34:29.960
it's probably a combination
of having access

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:31.440
to the right microbes.

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:33.910
And we can talk about what
that access looks like.

00:34:33.910 --> 00:34:35.570
It may look like
therapeutics in the future.

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:37.850
There are a lot of companies
working on creating cocktails

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:38.920
of healthy microbes.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:42.530
But it'll be a combination of
access to the right microbes

00:34:42.530 --> 00:34:46.930
and nourishing those microbes
with the proper diet.

00:34:46.930 --> 00:34:47.763
- Very interesting.

00:34:47.763 --> 00:34:49.680
This multi-generational
study reminds me of something

00:34:49.680 --> 00:34:51.488
that I was told early in my training,

00:34:51.488 --> 00:34:56.210
which was that it takes a long
time for a trait to evolve,

00:34:56.210 --> 00:34:59.000
but not a long time for traits to devolve.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:00.240
- Yeah, exactly.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:04.350
- Which generally is true
of human behavior too,

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:06.161
although it depends.

00:35:06.161 --> 00:35:07.757
We can all do better, nonetheless.

00:35:07.757 --> 00:35:08.750
- [Justin Sonnenburg] Very interesting.

00:35:08.750 --> 00:35:13.210
- So I have a puzzle
or a bit of a conundrum

00:35:14.310 --> 00:35:17.540
around this notion of
species of microbiota.

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:21.129
So if the pH, if the acidity

00:35:21.129 --> 00:35:23.770
differs along the digestive tract,

00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:26.110
but is more or less fixed
for a given location, right?

00:35:26.110 --> 00:35:27.310
I mean, unless something's really off,

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:29.920
the pH of the stomach is
within a particular range,

00:35:29.920 --> 00:35:32.550
and the intestines and so forth.

00:35:32.550 --> 00:35:36.980
And certain microbiota
thrive at a given station,

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:39.530
a given location along
the digestive tract,

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:42.693
and the pH is sort of fixed more or less.

00:35:43.590 --> 00:35:45.580
I'm trying to figure out,

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:47.950
what is allowing certain microbiota

00:35:47.950 --> 00:35:49.970
to stay in a given location?

00:35:49.970 --> 00:35:51.700
Why don't they migrate up or down?

00:35:51.700 --> 00:35:52.990
So are they pH sensitive,

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:56.310
and that's what they're
selecting for along the tract?

00:35:56.310 --> 00:36:01.090
And I'm also trying to figure
out how these changes in food

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:03.440
so robustly change the microbiome.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:05.030
The way you describe it
almost makes it sound

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:07.850
like food is the variable
that's going to dictate

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:09.780
the quality of the microbiome,

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:11.960
although I'm sure there
are other factors as well.

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:13.460
And then in the back of my mind,

00:36:13.460 --> 00:36:15.250
I don't know that I want
to ask this question,

00:36:15.250 --> 00:36:16.700
but I really want to ask this question,

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:19.770
which is, where are they in there exactly,

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:21.710
and why don't they all get flushed out?

00:36:21.710 --> 00:36:22.610
Right?
- Right.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:26.623
- If 30% of fecal matter is microbiota,

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:29.390
then where are they living?

00:36:29.390 --> 00:36:33.730
Are they along the lining,
and the little microvilli

00:36:33.730 --> 00:36:36.564
of the intestine, and
what are they attaching to

00:36:36.564 --> 00:36:38.170
and interacting with?

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:40.740
we know there are neurons in there,

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:41.940
especially within the stomach,

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:43.680
there's a lot of work now being done

00:36:43.680 --> 00:36:45.900
on the gut neurons and how they signal

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:47.380
to the brain and so forth.

00:36:47.380 --> 00:36:49.350
But who are they talking to in terms

00:36:49.350 --> 00:36:52.430
of the host cells?
- Yeah.

00:36:52.430 --> 00:36:54.090
- Because if it's just from food,

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:56.100
I imagine that they're in
there having their good time,

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:59.970
or not, and then some are
getting flushed out or not,

00:36:59.970 --> 00:37:01.890
but how do they actually stay in there?

00:37:01.890 --> 00:37:03.430
- Yeah.
- Who are they attaching to?

00:37:03.430 --> 00:37:05.850
What are they talking about?
What are they doing for fun?

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:06.683
- Yeah.
- And so forth.

00:37:06.683 --> 00:37:08.330
- Yeah, yeah. Super interesting.

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:11.790
So I'll come back to
the attachment question,

00:37:11.790 --> 00:37:13.750
and kind of why they don't get washed out,

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:15.830
'cause this is a super
fascinating question.

00:37:15.830 --> 00:37:19.389
And I think your initial point of like,

00:37:19.389 --> 00:37:23.410
the kind of regional
differences in what's happening

00:37:23.410 --> 00:37:25.440
in terms of physiology, biochemistry

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:27.650
along the length of the
gut is really interesting.

00:37:27.650 --> 00:37:29.320
There certainly is a pH gradient

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:30.590
along the length of the gut.

00:37:30.590 --> 00:37:33.680
There is actually
bicarbonate that's secreted

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:37.380
into the small intestine to
try to neutralize stomach acid.

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:39.511
There also is bile that's secreted.

00:37:39.511 --> 00:37:42.820
That creates a different
chemical environment in there,

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:45.490
bio-loving bacteria that kind of live

00:37:45.490 --> 00:37:47.030
in that region of the gut.

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:51.180
And then there is a nutrient gradient,

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:54.053
just because as food leaves the stomach,

00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:58.380
a lot of the simple
nutrients are absorbed.

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:01.860
And so you might see microbes
in the small intestine,

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:04.720
for instance, that are better
at consuming simple sugars,

00:38:04.720 --> 00:38:07.220
but you won't find many
microbes in the colon like that,

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:08.430
because all the simple sugars

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:10.280
have been depleted at that point.

00:38:10.280 --> 00:38:13.597
And then the immune system
is a big factor as well.

00:38:13.597 --> 00:38:16.390
And the immune system is incredibly active

00:38:16.390 --> 00:38:18.310
in the small intestine.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:20.170
The small intestine is
this really interesting

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:22.870
challenge for the host, because
it's a tissue that's been...

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:24.756
Its purpose is mainly absorptive.

00:38:24.756 --> 00:38:29.756
And so there has to be
flow of a lot of things,

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:35.090
a lot of nutrients from
the luminal contents

00:38:35.090 --> 00:38:36.700
into host cells.

00:38:36.700 --> 00:38:40.680
And so that means the barrier
can't be as fortified.

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:44.340
And so the immune system
is incredibly active

00:38:44.340 --> 00:38:46.830
in the small intestine to
make sure that microbes

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:49.910
aren't getting so close, and
if they are getting close,

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:51.860
there's a response to
them to put them back

00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:53.183
in their right location.

00:38:55.417 --> 00:39:00.417
And then along this whole kind
of architecture of the gut,

00:39:01.056 --> 00:39:04.010
there's the longitudinal gradients,

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:06.012
things like pH and so forth.

00:39:06.012 --> 00:39:09.500
And I should say that pH starts
to drop again in the colon,

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:12.210
because a lot of those
microbes are fermenting things

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:13.830
and producing acids.

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:17.930
And so you actually end up
with the pH starting to drop,

00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:19.857
not as low as the stomach,
but starting to drop again

00:39:19.857 --> 00:39:22.803
if there's a lot of fermentation
happening in the colon.

00:39:23.830 --> 00:39:25.730
In addition, you also have a gradient

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:28.740
from the host surface epithelium

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:31.660
out to the middle of the gut.

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:36.660
And that is likely the key for
what is retained in the gut

00:39:38.160 --> 00:39:41.160
and how the community isn't washed out.

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:44.620
So lining the gut, we
have epithelial cells.

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:46.625
In the small intestine,
they're largely absorbative.

00:39:46.625 --> 00:39:50.257
In the colon, there's a
lot of mucus production.

00:39:50.257 --> 00:39:53.620
And we also see this
in the small intestine,

00:39:53.620 --> 00:39:58.470
and this mucus lining is this
substance that we secrete,

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:01.000
largely made of carbohydrate actually.

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:04.960
And the purpose of that
is to keep microbes

00:40:04.960 --> 00:40:08.930
in the right spot and to allow nutrients

00:40:08.930 --> 00:40:11.840
and water to be absorbed
in the small intestine

00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:12.680
and large intestine.

00:40:12.680 --> 00:40:15.270
And so it's this mesh
work that is supposed

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:17.510
to keep out large things
like back to and lead,

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:19.733
and small things like nutrients.

00:40:20.840 --> 00:40:25.840
That mucus layer is, it
turns over more slowly

00:40:26.380 --> 00:40:29.440
than the luminal contents passing by.

00:40:29.440 --> 00:40:34.110
And so if a microbe learns to
hold onto that mucus layer,

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:39.110
it can actually resist the flow
of the contents of the gut.

00:40:39.710 --> 00:40:42.360
And so there there's
many microbes in the gut

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:44.580
that are not just good
at attaching to mucus,

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:48.360
but also good at nibbling
on it, at eating it.

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:49.950
And there are these bacteria,

00:40:49.950 --> 00:40:53.108
like Akkermansia
muciniphila, mucus-loving.

00:40:53.108 --> 00:40:56.580
One of its main things it
does is actually eat mucus

00:40:56.580 --> 00:40:58.127
in the gut, that's its lifestyle.

00:40:58.127 --> 00:41:03.127
And so there's an incredible
gradient of activity

00:41:04.060 --> 00:41:05.200
from the host tissue,

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:07.260
working your way out to
the middle of the gut.

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.350
What's amazing is some microbes actually

00:41:09.350 --> 00:41:11.110
do penetrate past the mucus,

00:41:11.110 --> 00:41:13.000
and there are these invaginations

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:15.200
in the intestine known as crypts,

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:16.996
actually where the stem cells live

00:41:16.996 --> 00:41:18.960
that produce the epithelium,

00:41:18.960 --> 00:41:20.790
and there are microbial communities

00:41:20.790 --> 00:41:22.720
that can form in those crypts.

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:24.973
And we don't know completely
what their function is,

00:41:24.973 --> 00:41:27.860
but we've done some studies
that appear to indicate

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:30.620
that if you can localize to a crypt,

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:32.882
you've hit the jackpot as a microbe

00:41:32.882 --> 00:41:36.480
for being able to maintain
dominance in the gut.

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:40.380
So if you sit in the crypt
and something similar to you,

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:42.920
another microbe that's similar
to you, comes into the gut,

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:45.390
you can actually exclude that microbe.

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:49.390
And the thinking is that
it can't find a spot

00:41:49.390 --> 00:41:52.080
to resist being washed out of the gut.

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:54.080
So there probably are these little niches

00:41:54.080 --> 00:41:55.930
close to host tissue in the mucus

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:57.454
that are absolutely essential

00:41:57.454 --> 00:42:00.284
for resisting getting washed out

00:42:00.284 --> 00:42:02.920
with the flow of all the contents.

00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:04.520
- Incredible.

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:06.650
That raises a question about two things

00:42:06.650 --> 00:42:08.990
that are reasonably popular.

00:42:08.990 --> 00:42:13.110
One is this notion of cleanses,
from either direction.

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:15.060
People will consume things by mouth to try

00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:17.150
and cleanse their digestive tract.

00:42:17.150 --> 00:42:18.570
This there's a long history of this.

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:20.300
I'm not recommending this.

00:42:20.300 --> 00:42:21.900
There's differing
opinions on whether or not

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:23.050
this is good or bad.

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:26.950
And the other is fasting
or time-restricted feeding.

00:42:26.950 --> 00:42:29.820
The reason I ask about
time-restricted feeding,

00:42:29.820 --> 00:42:32.970
is my understanding is
that after a prolonged

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:36.590
period of fasting, there's
some auto-absorption

00:42:36.590 --> 00:42:40.910
or digestion of one's own digestive tract,

00:42:40.910 --> 00:42:41.900
that then gets renewed.

00:42:41.900 --> 00:42:43.830
In other words, you're testing and stomach

00:42:43.830 --> 00:42:45.760
start eating its own lining to some extent

00:42:45.760 --> 00:42:47.160
in the absence of food.

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:49.270
So what do we know about cleanses?

00:42:49.270 --> 00:42:50.500
Oh, and then I suppose there's cleanses

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:51.910
from the other direction too, right?

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:55.780
Which, less popular, but I've
never run the statistics,

00:42:55.780 --> 00:42:59.183
but certainly exist out there.

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:03.750
What's the idea about cleanses and fasting

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:06.210
as it relates to the
health or the dysbiosis

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:07.540
of the microbiota?

00:43:07.540 --> 00:43:10.760
- Yeah, there hasn't been a
lot of high quality science

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:12.900
in this area, and so it's
really hard to conclude

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.327
whether these are good for
health or bad for health.

00:43:15.327 --> 00:43:17.680
I think the fasting...

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:21.194
We're in a really interesting situation

00:43:21.194 --> 00:43:23.120
in the industrialized world,

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:24.750
because we have so many problems

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:26.520
associated with our digestive tract,

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:30.160
and that probably has to do
with our highly processed diet

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:31.850
and perhaps having a microbiota

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:34.020
that's fairly perturbed as well.

00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:35.083
And so,

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:42.660
whether doing things like
this are good or bad,

00:43:44.385 --> 00:43:48.330
it's really hard to define,
because we may be starting off

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:50.430
in a fairly bad state anyway.

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:53.280
There's so many diseases
that we're dealing with,

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:56.140
metabolic syndrome,
inflammatory bowel disease,

00:43:56.140 --> 00:44:00.430
that just put a massive
portion of the population

00:44:00.430 --> 00:44:03.880
in a very different category
than people that are thinking

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:07.840
about how do I maintain health,
how do I live a long life,

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:10.040
from starting off in what we consider

00:44:10.040 --> 00:44:11.530
a fairly healthy state.

00:44:11.530 --> 00:44:16.530
And so things like fasting
and a lot of other therapies

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:19.092
that have been developed in the field,

00:44:19.092 --> 00:44:23.730
I think ketogenic diet may
be in this category as well.

00:44:23.730 --> 00:44:28.600
There can be tremendous benefits
in terms of their impact

00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:30.890
in the context of metabolic syndrome,

00:44:30.890 --> 00:44:33.313
and for people that are battling

00:44:33.313 --> 00:44:37.537
eating a continual bad diet
or something like that.

00:44:37.537 --> 00:44:38.750
- And adherence.

00:44:38.750 --> 00:44:41.150
I think one of the one of the
reasons for the popularity

00:44:41.150 --> 00:44:45.600
of intermittent fasting,
time-restricted feeding,

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:48.440
and what do they call them now?

00:44:48.440 --> 00:44:51.390
Exclusion diets, where you
entire entirely exclude meat,

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:53.440
or you entirely exclude
plants or whatever it is,

00:44:53.440 --> 00:44:56.943
is that adherence is sometimes
easier in the all or none.

00:44:58.185 --> 00:45:00.970
As neurobiologists, we think
of it as a go, no-go circuitry.

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:04.590
It's harder to make decisions,

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:06.780
nuanced decisions often,
about food than it is

00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:09.470
to just eliminate entire
categories of food.

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:11.490
Not eating, for many people-
- Yeah.

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:13.604
- Is easier than eating smaller portions.

00:45:13.604 --> 00:45:15.077
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:15.077 --> 00:45:16.690
Yeah, and-
- Yeah.

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:17.523
So some of it, I think,

00:45:17.523 --> 00:45:19.200
is neurobiological and psychological.

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:20.050
- Absolutely.

00:45:20.050 --> 00:45:22.640
And we've had gastroenterology
fellows in our lab

00:45:22.640 --> 00:45:24.530
that come in and we kind of...

00:45:24.530 --> 00:45:28.100
I think that to kind of
slice through the nuance

00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:30.150
of all this, there's a very simple recipe

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:33.110
and a really well
accepted, broad definition

00:45:33.110 --> 00:45:34.540
of what a healthy diet is.

00:45:34.540 --> 00:45:39.030
kind of the Mediterranean
diet, plant-based diet is...

00:45:39.030 --> 00:45:41.780
There's just a ton of data
that particularly people

00:45:41.780 --> 00:45:45.885
of European ancestry, but
there's a pretty broad acceptance

00:45:45.885 --> 00:45:49.410
that if you eat mostly
plants, for most people,

00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:52.849
that's going to be very
healthy, to the point where...

00:45:52.849 --> 00:45:55.530
A wonderful colleague of
ours, Christopher Gardner,

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:58.950
who's studied diet his whole life,

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:00.930
trying to establish
what a healthy diet is.

00:46:00.930 --> 00:46:03.230
And people was giving advice,
I saw him giving advice

00:46:03.230 --> 00:46:06.573
to a dietician who is
trying to get all the rules

00:46:06.573 --> 00:46:10.470
of what she should be
recommending to people

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:12.880
that she deals with that are
interested in a healthy diet.

00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:14.920
And she said, so the number
one, I'm going to say,

00:46:14.920 --> 00:46:17.877
plant-based fiber is
probably super important,

00:46:17.877 --> 00:46:20.080
and that should be very high on the list.

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:23.380
And she goes on to number
two and he said, stop.

00:46:23.380 --> 00:46:26.580
He said, if people do number one well,

00:46:26.580 --> 00:46:28.540
you don't need to know any other rules.

00:46:28.540 --> 00:46:30.840
I mean, it's basically
like if you can have

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.480
a high fiber, plant-based diet,

00:46:33.480 --> 00:46:34.829
for most people, at least,

00:46:34.829 --> 00:46:36.360
talking about the bucket of people

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:38.200
that are already in a healthy state,

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:39.870
you don't really need to
think about other things,

00:46:39.870 --> 00:46:41.350
because you can't eat too much meat,

00:46:41.350 --> 00:46:42.590
you can't eat too many sweets.

00:46:42.590 --> 00:46:45.384
You've already eaten a huge
amount of plant-based fiber.

00:46:45.384 --> 00:46:49.460
Your gut is full, you're
not going to be hungry.

00:46:49.460 --> 00:46:52.040
And it kind of takes care of worrying

00:46:52.040 --> 00:46:54.290
about what should I eat or
what, what shouldn't I eat.

00:46:54.290 --> 00:46:59.290
Just eat a ton of whole
grains, legumes, vegetables,

00:46:59.993 --> 00:47:02.590
fruit that's high fiber
based, not high sugar.

00:47:02.590 --> 00:47:05.660
- Does it completely exclude
meat, and fish and dairy?

00:47:05.660 --> 00:47:09.000
- And he was saying people can
add their own spins on this.

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:12.777
But I think that the main
rule is just start off with...

00:47:12.777 --> 00:47:14.180
And it kind of gets back to,

00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:15.853
to Michael Pollan's mantra,

00:47:17.630 --> 00:47:20.030
eat food, not too much, mostly plants.

00:47:20.030 --> 00:47:22.950
I think if you stick
with these simple rules,

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:24.951
and don't overthink,
like, should I have this?

00:47:24.951 --> 00:47:27.110
Can I eat eggs? Can I eat...

00:47:27.110 --> 00:47:28.760
Just kind of stick to these simple rules,

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:30.440
it makes it very approachable.

00:47:30.440 --> 00:47:32.650
But I agree, so these
gastroenterology fellows

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:34.570
that we've had in our lab say that they...

00:47:34.570 --> 00:47:35.403
It's really hard.

00:47:35.403 --> 00:47:36.630
We kind of say to them,

00:47:36.630 --> 00:47:38.560
why won't you give this dietary advice

00:47:38.560 --> 00:47:39.750
that's really well-known.

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:41.710
And they just said, well,
it's, it's really hard

00:47:41.710 --> 00:47:43.364
to get people to change their diet,

00:47:43.364 --> 00:47:47.210
unless you're doing either
a go, no-go sort of thing,

00:47:47.210 --> 00:47:49.030
or eliminating something.

00:47:49.030 --> 00:47:52.000
So if carrots are giving you
problems, don't eat carrots.

00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:55.530
And that's a very simple,
easy instruction to follow,

00:47:55.530 --> 00:47:58.940
but doesn't really deal
with the root problem

00:47:58.940 --> 00:48:00.890
of why can't you eat carrots,

00:48:00.890 --> 00:48:02.750
because you should be able to eat carrots.

00:48:02.750 --> 00:48:04.160
Most people can eat carrots.

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:08.830
And so I think that we...

00:48:10.360 --> 00:48:15.280
Yeah, when we're thinking
about things like fasting,

00:48:15.280 --> 00:48:18.150
and all these different dietary regimes

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:19.833
and cleanses that people do,

00:48:20.730 --> 00:48:22.610
we have to step back for
a moment and say, okay,

00:48:22.610 --> 00:48:25.230
well, what are really the big,

00:48:25.230 --> 00:48:27.620
high level rules that we should take home?

00:48:27.620 --> 00:48:29.850
And then if you are experiencing problems,

00:48:29.850 --> 00:48:31.590
and you want to think about
how to deal with them,

00:48:31.590 --> 00:48:33.289
it's good to go to an
evidence-based method

00:48:33.289 --> 00:48:35.910
where there's actually data to back it up.

00:48:35.910 --> 00:48:40.570
The data in the field really
shows that with like fasting,

00:48:40.570 --> 00:48:43.510
particularly if you go to
animals that hibernate,

00:48:43.510 --> 00:48:45.700
or things like that, where
there's really extended fasts,

00:48:45.700 --> 00:48:48.972
you actually have a microbiota come up

00:48:48.972 --> 00:48:51.445
that blooms in the absence

00:48:51.445 --> 00:48:53.969
of food coming in through diet

00:48:53.969 --> 00:48:56.961
that's really good at eating mucus.

00:48:56.961 --> 00:49:01.130
So you have bacteria
that's that specialize

00:49:01.130 --> 00:49:03.230
in eating nutrients derived from the host,

00:49:03.230 --> 00:49:05.560
because there's no other
nutrients to live on.

00:49:05.560 --> 00:49:08.420
Now, whether this is good
or bad, we don't know.

00:49:08.420 --> 00:49:13.420
But it seems like the
consumption of mucus in excess

00:49:15.440 --> 00:49:18.990
is a problem from the
standpoint of microbes

00:49:18.990 --> 00:49:21.680
getting too close to host tissue
and inciting inflammation,

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:23.280
which is what we see in animal models

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:25.770
when we deprive of dietary fiber.

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:28.460
We see these mucus
utilizers become abundant,

00:49:28.460 --> 00:49:30.737
and inflammatory markers start to come on.

00:49:30.737 --> 00:49:35.240
So fasting short term
might be fine, probably.

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:37.940
There's definitely benefits
that are seen metabolically.

00:49:37.940 --> 00:49:40.510
In terms of what it means
for long term health

00:49:40.510 --> 00:49:42.330
from the standpoint of the gut microbiota,

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:44.720
I would say we don't have
the answer to that yet.

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:48.840
In terms of the cleanses and
the flushes and all this,

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:51.070
personally, I think it's a terrible idea.

00:49:51.070 --> 00:49:53.752
I mean, we know that if...

00:49:53.752 --> 00:49:56.620
In studies that are being
done now to reprogram

00:49:56.620 --> 00:49:58.060
the gut microbiota to install

00:49:58.060 --> 00:50:00.360
a completely new microbial community,

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:02.500
the first step is to wash away

00:50:02.500 --> 00:50:04.900
the resident microbial
community that's there.

00:50:04.900 --> 00:50:07.060
So if you're in the process of acquiring

00:50:07.060 --> 00:50:09.760
a really good microbiota
and you know how to do that,

00:50:09.760 --> 00:50:12.170
then flushing everything out is great.

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:16.110
Otherwise, what is happening
is you're kind of leaving

00:50:16.110 --> 00:50:19.610
rebuilding of the community
to chance, like what is it?

00:50:19.610 --> 00:50:23.240
And so what microbes
are going to colonize?

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:24.380
Who's going to take up space

00:50:24.380 --> 00:50:26.610
after you do this flush or cleanse?

00:50:26.610 --> 00:50:30.100
And I think it's a little bit

00:50:30.100 --> 00:50:31.210
like playing Russian Roulette.

00:50:31.210 --> 00:50:34.070
You may end up with a
good microbial community

00:50:34.070 --> 00:50:36.070
in there afterwards, you may not.

00:50:36.070 --> 00:50:37.710
You certainly want to pay close attention

00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:39.900
to what you're eating while you're doing

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:41.380
the reconstitution of the community

00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:43.090
after you do something like that.

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:43.933
- Yeah.

00:50:43.933 --> 00:50:45.180
Thank you for that.

00:50:45.180 --> 00:50:46.550
I know a lot of people are interested

00:50:46.550 --> 00:50:48.470
in these kinds of elimination diets.

00:50:48.470 --> 00:50:52.040
And intermittent fasting/
time-restricted feeding

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:53.700
seems to be getting
some traction, in part,

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:57.810
because at some level we are
all doing this when we sleep.

00:50:57.810 --> 00:50:59.750
Most of us aren't eating
while we sleep anyway,

00:50:59.750 --> 00:51:03.830
and adjusting the numbers
seems more accessible

00:51:03.830 --> 00:51:04.663
for a lot of people.

00:51:04.663 --> 00:51:05.710
We have a lot of colleagues at Stanford

00:51:05.710 --> 00:51:08.950
who I know happen to of that regimen,

00:51:08.950 --> 00:51:10.790
or a time-restricted feeding regimen,

00:51:10.790 --> 00:51:15.420
but also who follow the more
traditional meal spacing

00:51:15.420 --> 00:51:17.694
as well, of course.

00:51:17.694 --> 00:51:20.340
One of the things that I wonder about

00:51:20.340 --> 00:51:23.163
as we talk about primarily
plant-based with some,

00:51:23.163 --> 00:51:25.090
what did you say the Pollan thing was?

00:51:25.090 --> 00:51:28.860
It was eat mostly plants
and then maybe some meat,

00:51:28.860 --> 00:51:30.100
but not too much, or not

00:51:30.100 --> 00:51:30.933
too much...
- Yeah, eat food,

00:51:30.933 --> 00:51:32.940
mostly plants, not too much.
- Got it.

00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:35.130
- Or sorry, eat food, not
too much, mostly plants.

00:51:35.130 --> 00:51:35.963
- Got it.

00:51:38.953 --> 00:51:40.460
I hear this again and again.

00:51:40.460 --> 00:51:43.507
I know there are a number of
people who do seem to do well

00:51:43.507 --> 00:51:48.240
on a lower carbohydrate, and
even some people who report

00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:51.600
feeling much better on a really strictly,

00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:53.540
almost meat, organ-only diet.

00:51:53.540 --> 00:51:55.550
And the only reason I raise is not...

00:51:55.550 --> 00:51:56.950
I don't participate in...

00:51:56.950 --> 00:52:01.080
I'm one of those omnivores that out there,

00:52:01.080 --> 00:52:04.560
I do eat some meat and
I do eat plants as well.

00:52:04.560 --> 00:52:06.770
But the reason I raise
this is that earlier

00:52:06.770 --> 00:52:09.070
you were talking about communities

00:52:09.070 --> 00:52:13.390
that may have microbiota
that are healthier than ours,

00:52:13.390 --> 00:52:15.010
or at least different than ours.

00:52:15.010 --> 00:52:16.486
And there are communities in the world

00:52:16.486 --> 00:52:20.620
that subsist largely on animal products,

00:52:20.620 --> 00:52:23.500
or for which unprocessed animal products

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:25.950
are considered the richest-nutrient foods

00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:27.130
in those communities.

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:29.225
Protein is very scarce in...

00:52:29.225 --> 00:52:31.940
Ancestrally, protein was more scarce,

00:52:31.940 --> 00:52:33.730
so eggs and meat and things of that sort.

00:52:33.730 --> 00:52:35.420
So could there be a genetic component?

00:52:35.420 --> 00:52:37.149
In other words, if we
fast forward 10 years

00:52:37.149 --> 00:52:38.880
and we actually can make sense

00:52:38.880 --> 00:52:40.310
of all this human genome stuff,

00:52:40.310 --> 00:52:45.171
are we going to find that someone
who has Scandinavian roots,

00:52:45.171 --> 00:52:47.220
or somebody who has South American roots,

00:52:47.220 --> 00:52:49.740
or somebody had descended
from a different tribe,

00:52:49.740 --> 00:52:52.590
will do better on one
particular diet versus another,

00:52:52.590 --> 00:52:56.360
and thereby, or I should say,
and in parallel with that,

00:52:56.360 --> 00:52:57.510
that their gut microbiome

00:52:57.510 --> 00:52:59.290
will have different signatures that are...

00:52:59.290 --> 00:53:02.080
So your microbiome might thrive on plants

00:53:02.080 --> 00:53:04.230
and mine might thrive on organ meats.

00:53:04.230 --> 00:53:06.518
And as I say this, I'm not a
big consumer of organ meats.

00:53:06.518 --> 00:53:09.330
I'm just laying this
out for sake of example.

00:53:09.330 --> 00:53:10.500
- Yeah.

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:11.840
Yeah, great.

00:53:11.840 --> 00:53:12.673
So,

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:18.930
a few notes, the first one
has to do with carbohydrates

00:53:18.930 --> 00:53:20.340
and restriction of carbohydrates,

00:53:20.340 --> 00:53:21.790
and some people feeling healthier

00:53:21.790 --> 00:53:23.790
when they cut carbohydrates out.

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:25.790
My guess is...

00:53:25.790 --> 00:53:29.825
This is my theory to be tested,

00:53:29.825 --> 00:53:33.360
that people feel better
cutting carbohydrates out,

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:37.500
because the diet that we
eat in the United States

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:39.124
and in industrialized countries,

00:53:39.124 --> 00:53:42.290
the carbohydrates are largely crap.

00:53:42.290 --> 00:53:43.476
- Processed.
- They're processed.

00:53:43.476 --> 00:53:44.850
- Right.
- It's like starch,

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:46.390
simple sugar.

00:53:46.390 --> 00:53:49.130
It's things that contribute
to glycemic index.

00:53:49.130 --> 00:53:52.210
It's these sugars that we eat.

00:53:52.210 --> 00:53:53.867
They make it to our small intestine,

00:53:53.867 --> 00:53:56.760
they get chopped up into simple sugars,

00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:58.170
absorbed into our bloodstream,

00:53:58.170 --> 00:53:59.810
and we have a ton of glucose then

00:53:59.810 --> 00:54:02.440
coursing through our
veins, which we know is bad

00:54:02.440 --> 00:54:04.573
and can lead to things like diabetes.

00:54:05.750 --> 00:54:08.920
If the carbohydrates that were in our diet

00:54:08.920 --> 00:54:12.810
were complex carbohydrates, dietary fiber,

00:54:12.810 --> 00:54:15.650
and we like to refer to
the subset of dietary fiber

00:54:15.650 --> 00:54:18.090
that the microbiota can actually access

00:54:18.090 --> 00:54:21.560
as microbiota-accessible carbohydrates.

00:54:21.560 --> 00:54:22.960
And the reason that we like that term

00:54:22.960 --> 00:54:24.840
is it has the word carbohydrate in it.

00:54:24.840 --> 00:54:28.280
And it's to point out that
not all carbs are bad,

00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:30.434
it's just, there are bad carbs or carbs,

00:54:30.434 --> 00:54:32.100
or carbs that are bad if you consume them

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:33.140
in too high quantity,

00:54:33.140 --> 00:54:36.422
things like table sugar
and simple starches,

00:54:36.422 --> 00:54:38.500
but there are good carbs as well.

00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:40.930
And these microbiota-accessible
carbohydrates

00:54:40.930 --> 00:54:43.000
are the complex ones that we can't digest,

00:54:43.000 --> 00:54:46.200
and fuel our got microbiota,

00:54:46.200 --> 00:54:48.000
our gut microbiota can ferment them.

00:54:50.931 --> 00:54:55.610
I think we probably
all would be better off

00:54:55.610 --> 00:54:59.030
with less of the carbs that
we're typically served,

00:54:59.030 --> 00:55:03.054
but most of us, and probably
the vast majority of us

00:55:03.054 --> 00:55:06.820
would be better off by
consuming a lot more carbs

00:55:06.820 --> 00:55:09.700
that were complex, that
we're microbiotic-accessible.

00:55:09.700 --> 00:55:11.620
And I'll come back to why that's important

00:55:11.620 --> 00:55:12.680
in terms of our biology.

00:55:12.680 --> 00:55:15.600
There's some mechanisms
that are known as to why

00:55:15.600 --> 00:55:17.470
those complex carbohydrates
are so important

00:55:17.470 --> 00:55:19.573
for our health, for most of us.

00:55:20.494 --> 00:55:23.860
I think this aspect of human
genetic adaptation to diet

00:55:23.860 --> 00:55:25.450
is super interesting.

00:55:25.450 --> 00:55:28.326
And then layer on top
of that got microbiota

00:55:28.326 --> 00:55:31.410
adaptation to diet, which
is another layer of this

00:55:31.410 --> 00:55:33.200
that is also fascinating.

00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:35.850
It's very clear that over
very short periods of time,

00:55:35.850 --> 00:55:38.610
humans can adapt to
differences in their diet,

00:55:38.610 --> 00:55:40.250
lactase persistence as kind of

00:55:40.250 --> 00:55:41.830
the classic example of this.

00:55:41.830 --> 00:55:44.083
Just over the past 10,000 years,

00:55:45.439 --> 00:55:49.030
certain groups of humans have adapted

00:55:49.030 --> 00:55:53.670
to being able to consume
dairy by taking this enzyme,

00:55:53.670 --> 00:55:55.700
lactase, that normally is just expressed

00:55:55.700 --> 00:55:57.831
in most of the world's
population early in life

00:55:57.831 --> 00:56:02.562
to be able to metabolize
lactose in breast milk.

00:56:02.562 --> 00:56:05.870
By extending the expression
of that throughout life,

00:56:05.870 --> 00:56:09.080
now you can consume milk
for your whole life.

00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:12.500
And so that is an example
of specific populations

00:56:12.500 --> 00:56:16.660
of human genome,
genetically adapting to diet

00:56:16.660 --> 00:56:17.960
in a very short period of time.

00:56:17.960 --> 00:56:20.400
And there are other examples
of this, and undoubtedly,

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:22.490
this has happened throughout the world

00:56:22.490 --> 00:56:23.780
to various aspects of diets.

00:56:23.780 --> 00:56:26.110
So certainly it's important to remember

00:56:26.110 --> 00:56:30.010
that there will be different diets

00:56:30.010 --> 00:56:31.930
that are better for different groups,

00:56:31.930 --> 00:56:35.900
based on what genes you harbor and have

00:56:35.900 --> 00:56:37.490
in your human genome.

00:56:37.490 --> 00:56:40.173
The other aspect on top of that is that,

00:56:41.570 --> 00:56:46.420
there are good examples
of the gut microbiome

00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:49.110
adapting to cultural differences in diet.

00:56:49.110 --> 00:56:50.619
And the classic example of this

00:56:50.619 --> 00:56:54.040
is the degradation of seaweed.

00:56:54.040 --> 00:56:57.890
So we know that most
Americans, if you eat sushi,

00:56:57.890 --> 00:57:02.890
and there's nori there and
you eat some of the seaweed,

00:57:02.938 --> 00:57:06.920
it has a dietary fiber
in it known as porphyrin.

00:57:06.920 --> 00:57:10.200
That porphyrin will shoot
through most of us untransformed.

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:11.478
Inert substance.

00:57:11.478 --> 00:57:13.610
It'll do other things like retain water,

00:57:13.610 --> 00:57:15.690
and serve as kind of
something like cellulose,

00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:18.820
not be fermented at a high level.

00:57:18.820 --> 00:57:20.320
If somebody from Southeast Asia

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:23.200
that's always consumed seaweed,

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:25.560
and is part of a culture
that consumes seaweed,

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:27.897
eats seaweed, they have a gut microbe

00:57:27.897 --> 00:57:31.210
that can now metabolize porphyrin.

00:57:31.210 --> 00:57:34.570
And so there are these very
specific gene transfer events

00:57:34.570 --> 00:57:37.470
where the genes for
breaking down porphyrin

00:57:37.470 --> 00:57:40.834
have been imported into the microbiome

00:57:40.834 --> 00:57:45.390
of many people in Southeast Asia to...

00:57:45.390 --> 00:57:47.520
We can think of it as
helping digest porphyrin,

00:57:47.520 --> 00:57:50.140
but it's really just a
microbe that's found a niche,

00:57:50.140 --> 00:57:52.440
found a way to make a living in the gut

00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:55.480
by consuming something that's
common in the diet there.

00:57:55.480 --> 00:57:56.730
So there are these different layers,

00:57:56.730 --> 00:57:58.240
there are human genetic adaptations,

00:57:58.240 --> 00:58:00.776
and there are microbiome
adaptations that are cultural,

00:58:00.776 --> 00:58:03.980
and based on people's geographic location.

00:58:03.980 --> 00:58:07.350
But there's no escaping the fact

00:58:07.350 --> 00:58:09.650
that for much of human evolution,

00:58:09.650 --> 00:58:13.790
the vast majority of people
that are on this planet

00:58:13.790 --> 00:58:18.650
had ancestors that were
hunter-gatherers, foraging,

00:58:18.650 --> 00:58:22.120
consuming huge quantities
of plant material,

00:58:22.120 --> 00:58:24.370
just because that's what was there.

00:58:24.370 --> 00:58:26.240
And so one of the groups that we study,

00:58:26.240 --> 00:58:28.650
the Hadza hunter-gatherers in Africa,

00:58:28.650 --> 00:58:33.650
and I should take a moment
just to say that our research,

00:58:33.974 --> 00:58:36.820
and research of many people
in our field and other fields

00:58:36.820 --> 00:58:39.960
rely on study of indigenous communities.

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:43.670
And it's really important to
think of these communities,

00:58:43.670 --> 00:58:48.530
as our equals, they're
modern people on the planet.

00:58:48.530 --> 00:58:50.900
They have interesting
lifestyles that are informative

00:58:50.900 --> 00:58:54.530
with regard to certain
aspects of human biology,

00:58:54.530 --> 00:58:56.620
but in many cases, they also are

00:58:57.660 --> 00:58:59.920
leading a vulnerable existence.

00:58:59.920 --> 00:59:02.640
And so we really take great
care in our research program,

00:59:02.640 --> 00:59:04.230
and it's important for people to realize

00:59:04.230 --> 00:59:07.370
that these populations
take part in our research,

00:59:07.370 --> 00:59:09.660
because they're wonderful
research partners.

00:59:09.660 --> 00:59:12.350
And we need to be mindful
of kind of thinking

00:59:12.350 --> 00:59:17.032
about how, yeah, both we talk
about them and use our data

00:59:17.032 --> 00:59:21.080
that has been gained through
their generous contribution

00:59:21.080 --> 00:59:22.720
to our research program.

00:59:22.720 --> 00:59:24.690
The Hadza hunter-gatherers,
it's estimated,

00:59:24.690 --> 00:59:27.700
consume on the order 100 to 150 grams

00:59:27.700 --> 00:59:29.349
of dietary fiber per day,

00:59:29.349 --> 00:59:32.910
and that's in stark contrast
to the typical American

00:59:32.910 --> 00:59:35.520
that consumes about 15 grams.

00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:38.299
So somewhere, a seven to tenfold decrease

00:59:38.299 --> 00:59:41.672
in the main nutrient that
feeds are got microbiome

00:59:41.672 --> 00:59:43.730
in the American diet.

00:59:43.730 --> 00:59:48.010
The Hadza are one example.

00:59:48.010 --> 00:59:50.740
There are many different
foraging populations,

00:59:50.740 --> 00:59:53.558
but the vast majority of these populations

00:59:53.558 --> 00:59:56.720
consume huge amounts of dietary fiber,

00:59:56.720 --> 01:00:00.460
because plants are the
reliable, consistent source.

01:00:00.460 --> 01:00:03.910
If you, as a hunter-gatherer go on a hunt,

01:00:03.910 --> 01:00:06.440
usually that hunt is unsuccessful.

01:00:06.440 --> 01:00:10.970
I think the data that one out
of 20 hunts are successful

01:00:10.970 --> 01:00:13.740
in landing actually
big game for the Hadza.

01:00:13.740 --> 01:00:17.530
They have birds that they
shoot and small animals,

01:00:17.530 --> 01:00:19.840
but quite often, day after day,

01:00:19.840 --> 01:00:24.840
they're relying upon berries,
tubers, baobab fruit.

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:27.600
They're relying on the
plants in their environment.

01:00:27.600 --> 01:00:30.740
And actually if you go
to the data and look

01:00:30.740 --> 01:00:33.370
at what their fruit food preferences are,

01:00:33.370 --> 01:00:35.930
their food preferences are
actually meat and honey.

01:00:35.930 --> 01:00:39.380
So they don't eat a high fiber
diet because they love fiber.

01:00:39.380 --> 01:00:40.530
They eat a high fiber diet,

01:00:40.530 --> 01:00:43.183
because that's what's
available and consistent

01:00:43.183 --> 01:00:45.840
for them to survive.

01:00:45.840 --> 01:00:49.010
But our brains are wired
for caloric density.

01:00:49.010 --> 01:00:51.690
And so if you took a Hadza
and put them in a restaurant

01:00:51.690 --> 01:00:52.910
in the United States,

01:00:52.910 --> 01:00:55.240
they would make the same
crappy decisions that we make,

01:00:55.240 --> 01:00:59.510
because we all want sugar
and fat and calories.

01:00:59.510 --> 01:01:01.440
It's how our brain is wired.

01:01:01.440 --> 01:01:03.770
- And protein and fat
for brain development,

01:01:03.770 --> 01:01:04.760
as far as we know, right?

01:01:04.760 --> 01:01:06.840
So it sounds like the Hadza,

01:01:06.840 --> 01:01:08.350
I hope I'm pronouncing that correctly,

01:01:08.350 --> 01:01:12.060
you said would prefer
to eat meat and honey,

01:01:12.060 --> 01:01:15.570
but they happen to consume
a lot of plant fiber

01:01:15.570 --> 01:01:17.420
as a consequence of what's available.

01:01:18.753 --> 01:01:23.540
One of the questions I have
as it relates to all of this,

01:01:23.540 --> 01:01:28.540
is it sounds to me like
there is no question

01:01:29.020 --> 01:01:34.020
from the pure vegan all the
way to the extreme opposite,

01:01:34.630 --> 01:01:37.055
which would be pure meat diet,

01:01:37.055 --> 01:01:40.150
that avoiding processed
foods is a good idea.

01:01:40.150 --> 01:01:42.190
- Yeah.
- Or heavily processed foods,

01:01:42.190 --> 01:01:45.420
in general and I mean, not
that the occasional consumption

01:01:45.420 --> 01:01:47.900
is necessarily bad.

01:01:47.900 --> 01:01:50.410
But whether or not one is thinking

01:01:50.410 --> 01:01:52.480
about one macronutrient
profile or another,

01:01:52.480 --> 01:01:54.820
it sounds like consuming processed foods

01:01:54.820 --> 01:01:56.730
is just bad for the microbiome.

01:01:56.730 --> 01:01:58.540
Can we say that categorically?

01:01:58.540 --> 01:01:59.412
For sure, yeah, yeah.
- Okay.

01:01:59.412 --> 01:02:00.554
- Absolutely.

01:02:00.554 --> 01:02:02.710
- So you're a low carb person,
you're a zero carb person,

01:02:02.710 --> 01:02:04.540
you're extreme vegan, no meat.

01:02:04.540 --> 01:02:06.560
Whether or not you're
all meat, organ meat.

01:02:06.560 --> 01:02:10.150
Sounds to me as if the number one thing,

01:02:10.150 --> 01:02:14.840
maybe even, dare I say above
Chris's point about plants,

01:02:14.840 --> 01:02:17.290
although I'm not going to challenge
Chris Gardner on nutrition,

01:02:17.290 --> 01:02:20.210
I would be way outside
the lane lines to do that.

01:02:20.210 --> 01:02:22.540
But is it to avoiding processed foods?

01:02:22.540 --> 01:02:23.960
- Yeah.
- Is paramount.

01:02:23.960 --> 01:02:26.080
- Yeah, and I think that's
completely compatible

01:02:26.080 --> 01:02:27.020
with what Christopher was saying.

01:02:27.020 --> 01:02:30.110
He was saying, if you
prioritize getting a huge amount

01:02:30.110 --> 01:02:35.010
of whole, plant-based food
with a lot of fiber first,

01:02:35.010 --> 01:02:36.050
you're not going to have room

01:02:36.050 --> 01:02:37.281
for eating a lot of processed food.

01:02:37.281 --> 01:02:38.114
- [Andrew Huberman] Yeah, yeah.

01:02:38.114 --> 01:02:40.130
- So it's kind of the same
as avoiding processed food.

01:02:40.130 --> 01:02:42.350
So I think that those are
exactly the same rule,

01:02:42.350 --> 01:02:44.437
and I think that you're exactly right.

01:02:44.437 --> 01:02:46.194
And we can break down...

01:02:46.194 --> 01:02:48.460
There's a lot of data of
why different components

01:02:48.460 --> 01:02:50.060
of processed food are so bad for us,

01:02:50.060 --> 01:02:51.500
and so bad for our microbiome.

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:53.730
And I can talk about a
few examples of that.

01:02:53.730 --> 01:02:57.840
But the flip side of this
is this mechanism of...

01:02:57.840 --> 01:03:01.090
And again, thinking about the
spectrum of a plant-based diet

01:03:01.090 --> 01:03:03.122
versus a meat-based diet.

01:03:03.122 --> 01:03:07.360
There's a lot of data to tell us

01:03:07.360 --> 01:03:10.650
that a meat or ketogenic, or high fat diet

01:03:10.650 --> 01:03:13.250
may have big benefits in terms

01:03:13.250 --> 01:03:14.640
of short term metabolic health.

01:03:14.640 --> 01:03:16.871
That's typically how people
think about that diet.

01:03:16.871 --> 01:03:18.770
There's also a lot of heart disease

01:03:18.770 --> 01:03:20.020
that's linked with that as well.

01:03:20.020 --> 01:03:21.210
There's good literature for that,

01:03:21.210 --> 01:03:25.080
which is something for people
to look at and be aware of.

01:03:25.080 --> 01:03:29.069
The plant-based diet, if you're
eating a bunch of complex

01:03:29.069 --> 01:03:31.890
fibers that feed your gut microbiota,

01:03:31.890 --> 01:03:34.720
your gut microbiota
produces these substances

01:03:34.720 --> 01:03:37.520
called short-chain fatty
acids, things like butyrate.

01:03:37.520 --> 01:03:40.220
And it's known that these
short-chain fatty acids

01:03:40.220 --> 01:03:41.647
play really essential components,

01:03:41.647 --> 01:03:45.650
both in terms of fueling
colonocytes, enforcing the barrier,

01:03:45.650 --> 01:03:48.230
keeping inflammation low,
regulating the immune system,

01:03:48.230 --> 01:03:49.840
regulating metabolism.

01:03:49.840 --> 01:03:53.490
And so a lot of people
think of dietary fiber

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:55.550
as this inert substance
that passes through,

01:03:55.550 --> 01:03:57.410
makes this feel full
maybe for a little bit,

01:03:57.410 --> 01:03:59.160
but we get hungry afterwards right away.

01:03:59.160 --> 01:04:00.440
If you're eating a lot of fiber

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:01.822
that's feeding your gut microbiota,

01:04:01.822 --> 01:04:05.738
your gut microbiota is just
producing this vast array

01:04:05.738 --> 01:04:08.530
of fermentation end products

01:04:08.530 --> 01:04:11.020
that then get absorbed
into our bloodstream,

01:04:11.020 --> 01:04:13.570
and have all of these
tremendous, cascading effects

01:04:13.570 --> 01:04:17.140
that appear to be largely
beneficial on our biology.

01:04:17.140 --> 01:04:19.930
And so to think about that
paradigm of simple carbs,

01:04:19.930 --> 01:04:22.630
versus complex carbs, in
the case of simple carbs,

01:04:22.630 --> 01:04:24.100
you end up with high blood sugar,

01:04:24.100 --> 01:04:26.780
something that will spike your insulin,

01:04:26.780 --> 01:04:29.440
and have all kinds of
weird, metabolic effects.

01:04:29.440 --> 01:04:31.145
In the case of complex carbohydrates,

01:04:31.145 --> 01:04:34.110
you'll end up with very low blood sugar,

01:04:34.110 --> 01:04:36.138
because most of those
have low glycemic index

01:04:36.138 --> 01:04:38.440
and a bunch of short-chain fatty acids

01:04:38.440 --> 01:04:39.900
that are having regulatory roles.

01:04:39.900 --> 01:04:42.890
So just to round out that topic,

01:04:42.890 --> 01:04:45.131
I think there is a reason to think that,

01:04:45.131 --> 01:04:48.480
maybe not appropriate for
absolutely everyone out there,

01:04:48.480 --> 01:04:51.200
but I think the vast majority of people,

01:04:51.200 --> 01:04:54.100
particularly given the statistics
of what we know people eat

01:04:54.100 --> 01:04:56.920
in the United States and in
industrialized countries,

01:04:56.920 --> 01:04:59.760
most people would reap
tremendous health benefits

01:04:59.760 --> 01:05:03.650
from eating more whole,
plant-based dietary fiber.

01:05:03.650 --> 01:05:06.380
Now, processed foods, I
think, is this other dimension

01:05:06.380 --> 01:05:08.220
where you have all of
these weird chemicals,

01:05:08.220 --> 01:05:11.757
artificial sweeteners, weird fats,

01:05:11.757 --> 01:05:14.630
a lot of refined, simple nutrients.

01:05:14.630 --> 01:05:16.130
The simple nutrients we've talked about,

01:05:16.130 --> 01:05:18.560
but we know that for instance,
artificial sweeteners

01:05:18.560 --> 01:05:21.270
can have a massive negative
impact on the gut microbiome

01:05:21.270 --> 01:05:24.000
and can lead us towards
metabolic syndrome, actually.

01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:25.430
There's been beautiful work

01:05:25.430 --> 01:05:27.250
out of the Weizmann Institute on this.

01:05:27.250 --> 01:05:28.730
And then emulsifiers,

01:05:28.730 --> 01:05:30.877
these compounds that are
put in processed foods

01:05:30.877 --> 01:05:34.230
to help them maintain shelf stability

01:05:34.230 --> 01:05:35.610
so things don't separate.

01:05:35.610 --> 01:05:39.270
And so all the the moisture content

01:05:39.270 --> 01:05:41.600
is retained appropriately.

01:05:41.600 --> 01:05:45.090
Many of these are known to
disrupt the mucus layer,

01:05:45.090 --> 01:05:47.300
and as soon as you start
disrupting that barrier,

01:05:47.300 --> 01:05:49.308
that can lead you in the
direction of inflammation

01:05:49.308 --> 01:05:50.980
and in animal models,

01:05:50.980 --> 01:05:53.420
we know that can lead towards
metabolic syndrome as well.

01:05:53.420 --> 01:05:57.420
So there's components of
processed food, that are,

01:05:57.420 --> 01:05:59.250
when studied in isolation known to have

01:05:59.250 --> 01:06:02.447
a direct negative impact on
gut biology in the microbiota.

01:06:02.447 --> 01:06:05.586
- Yeah, the mention of artificial
sweeteners interesting.

01:06:05.586 --> 01:06:09.150
I confess it's a third rail on...

01:06:09.150 --> 01:06:09.983
Talking about artificial sweeteners,

01:06:09.983 --> 01:06:13.750
there are two camps it
seems, or at least two camps.

01:06:13.750 --> 01:06:15.700
One that say artificial sweeteners

01:06:15.700 --> 01:06:17.240
are not detrimental at all.

01:06:17.240 --> 01:06:18.710
Another that says
they're very detrimental,

01:06:18.710 --> 01:06:20.030
mainly based on the mouse studies.

01:06:20.030 --> 01:06:21.800
And then there are people
in the middle that are...

01:06:21.800 --> 01:06:22.700
I put myself in that category.

01:06:22.700 --> 01:06:25.040
I drink the occasional diet soda,

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:26.550
I don't consume them in large volume,

01:06:26.550 --> 01:06:28.350
but I'm sort of in the middle there.

01:06:29.450 --> 01:06:30.930
And so I just throw that out there,

01:06:30.930 --> 01:06:33.880
because I know immediately
people are jumping on that,

01:06:33.880 --> 01:06:36.028
but I will just mention
there's some recent data

01:06:36.028 --> 01:06:38.780
out of Diego Bohorquez's
lab at Duke University

01:06:38.780 --> 01:06:40.270
that the neurons that
live in the gut mucose

01:06:40.270 --> 01:06:43.160
of these neuro pod cells
can actually distinguish

01:06:43.160 --> 01:06:46.740
between artificial and true sweet...

01:06:46.740 --> 01:06:49.401
Sugar versus artificial sweeteners.

01:06:49.401 --> 01:06:52.260
They send different
patterns of neural signals

01:06:52.260 --> 01:06:54.440
up to the brain, and the brain circuitry

01:06:54.440 --> 01:06:55.810
seems strongly impacted.

01:06:55.810 --> 01:06:57.650
So I think that as the data emerge,

01:06:57.650 --> 01:07:00.390
we're hearing more and more of
these artificial sweeteners.

01:07:00.390 --> 01:07:03.010
Either are problematic
or at least are signaling

01:07:03.010 --> 01:07:05.930
different events in the gut.

01:07:05.930 --> 01:07:08.532
I do want to make sure that we distinguish

01:07:08.532 --> 01:07:10.120
artificial sweeteners

01:07:10.120 --> 01:07:12.997
from non-caloric plant-based sweeteners.

01:07:12.997 --> 01:07:14.270
- Yeah.
- And this is based

01:07:14.270 --> 01:07:16.010
on a mistake that I've
made over and over again

01:07:16.010 --> 01:07:18.230
on the podcast where
I'll just kind of lump

01:07:18.230 --> 01:07:21.145
artificial sweeten into one big category,

01:07:21.145 --> 01:07:23.190
and then I'll mention stevia.

01:07:23.190 --> 01:07:24.970
So what about plant-based sweeteners

01:07:24.970 --> 01:07:26.590
that are not artificial,

01:07:26.590 --> 01:07:28.140
they weren't manufactured in a laboratory,

01:07:28.140 --> 01:07:30.848
like saccharin or sucralose, or aspartame?

01:07:30.848 --> 01:07:33.130
Do we know anything about plant-based,

01:07:33.130 --> 01:07:36.700
non caloric sweeteners or
low caloric sweeteners?

01:07:36.700 --> 01:07:37.600
- Very little.

01:07:37.600 --> 01:07:41.210
A lot of those have a lot
more bang for the buck.

01:07:41.210 --> 01:07:42.540
They're incredibly sweet,

01:07:42.540 --> 01:07:44.410
so it takes a really small amount for them

01:07:44.410 --> 01:07:46.330
to trigger a huge amount of sweetness.

01:07:46.330 --> 01:07:50.787
And so it's, depending upon
the mechanism of action

01:07:50.787 --> 01:07:54.022
by which these sweeteners
that are not sugar

01:07:54.022 --> 01:07:57.400
are impacting our biology.

01:07:57.400 --> 01:08:02.270
It may be that those are
actually less negative

01:08:02.270 --> 01:08:05.820
or more healthy than the
ones that are artificial,

01:08:05.820 --> 01:08:08.510
just because it requires
less of them in the food

01:08:08.510 --> 01:08:10.320
for us to perceive that sweet taste.

01:08:10.320 --> 01:08:12.460
It may also be that because they're...

01:08:12.460 --> 01:08:15.050
I don't think that everything
is that's natural is better,

01:08:15.050 --> 01:08:17.073
necessarily than things
that are artificial,

01:08:17.073 --> 01:08:21.370
but it may be that because of
kind of evolutionary exposure

01:08:21.370 --> 01:08:23.092
to these compounds in our diet,

01:08:23.092 --> 01:08:25.230
historically there are, I think,

01:08:25.230 --> 01:08:27.580
traditional populations that
use these, for instance,

01:08:27.580 --> 01:08:32.580
to sweeten different foods,
that our bodies just know

01:08:33.440 --> 01:08:35.080
how to deal with those compounds better

01:08:35.080 --> 01:08:36.781
than the ones that are synthetic.

01:08:36.781 --> 01:08:39.980
But I think the studies
still need to be done.

01:08:39.980 --> 01:08:42.500
- Do you actively avoid
artificial sweeteners?

01:08:42.500 --> 01:08:44.780
Sucralose, aspartame, saccharin?

01:08:44.780 --> 01:08:46.850
You personally.
- Yeah.

01:08:46.850 --> 01:08:50.560
So I do, I avoid them, but I'm not...

01:08:52.040 --> 01:08:55.970
So I work closely with my
wife, Erica, as you know.

01:08:55.970 --> 01:09:00.477
We were in the lab together
and we wrote this book,

01:09:00.477 --> 01:09:03.520
"The Good Gut," where we
kind of document our journey

01:09:03.520 --> 01:09:07.420
in changing our lifestyle,
dietary habits, choices we make

01:09:07.420 --> 01:09:09.310
based on the research as
we've gotten to know it

01:09:09.310 --> 01:09:11.840
in the gut microbiota
over the past 15 years.

01:09:11.840 --> 01:09:16.840
And I think that one of the
lessons that we've learned

01:09:17.970 --> 01:09:21.570
is that just doing things in moderation

01:09:21.570 --> 01:09:25.250
makes it a lot easier,
and doing things slowly

01:09:25.250 --> 01:09:26.200
makes it a lot easier.

01:09:26.200 --> 01:09:29.630
So there are very few rules that I have

01:09:29.630 --> 01:09:30.730
that are hard and fast.

01:09:30.730 --> 01:09:32.600
I'm a pretty flexible eater.

01:09:32.600 --> 01:09:35.500
I don't believe that having a diet Coke

01:09:35.500 --> 01:09:39.310
will somehow cascade into
some terrible disease

01:09:39.310 --> 01:09:40.860
or something like that.

01:09:40.860 --> 01:09:43.510
I try to avoid them, I don't
really like the flavor of them.

01:09:43.510 --> 01:09:47.110
I'm super sensitive to
the nuances of the flavor,

01:09:47.110 --> 01:09:49.500
even with the stevia and mogrosides

01:09:49.500 --> 01:09:50.815
from monk fruit and stuff like that.

01:09:50.815 --> 01:09:53.513
The off flavors are really
hard for me to deal with.

01:09:55.895 --> 01:09:58.830
But also in this journey
of changing our diet...

01:09:58.830 --> 01:10:01.540
Like when we started off
in microbiome research,

01:10:01.540 --> 01:10:04.270
I was in the habit of in the
afternoons having a sweet,

01:10:04.270 --> 01:10:06.400
a muffin or a cookie,
or something like that.

01:10:06.400 --> 01:10:09.770
And when we started to realize
that we should be eating

01:10:09.770 --> 01:10:12.201
less sweets and eating more dietary fiber,

01:10:12.201 --> 01:10:14.960
this was an incredibly
difficult change for me to make.

01:10:14.960 --> 01:10:18.310
I was just wired to kind of crave this-

01:10:18.310 --> 01:10:19.920
- Classic scientist.

01:10:19.920 --> 01:10:22.505
Scientists love the pastry in
the afternoon and the coffee.

01:10:22.505 --> 01:10:24.060
- Yeah, yeah.
- And in the old days

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:25.710
it used to be a cigarette too.

01:10:25.710 --> 01:10:27.103
- Right, exactly.
- When I started my training,

01:10:27.103 --> 01:10:29.120
a lot of people still smoked

01:10:29.120 --> 01:10:29.953
- Yeah, right.

01:10:29.953 --> 01:10:32.210
- And it was only during
my post-doctoral training

01:10:32.210 --> 01:10:33.970
that they eliminate smoking on campuses,

01:10:33.970 --> 01:10:37.061
and productivity took
a trough for a while,

01:10:37.061 --> 01:10:39.310
until these people developed other tools

01:10:39.310 --> 01:10:42.370
to focus their attention.
- Exactly, exactly.

01:10:42.370 --> 01:10:44.240
So there is this kind of need,

01:10:44.240 --> 01:10:46.080
and then once you have
an ingrained behavior,

01:10:46.080 --> 01:10:47.440
and maybe things that are addictive,

01:10:47.440 --> 01:10:50.499
it becomes incredibly
difficult to break that habit.

01:10:50.499 --> 01:10:55.499
And so I would say, gradually
over the course of like,

01:10:55.721 --> 01:11:00.721
five or more years, we
have migrated our diet

01:11:00.850 --> 01:11:05.850
away from sweet foods to
things that are less sweet.

01:11:05.860 --> 01:11:09.247
And it's been a journey,
it's been a slow process,

01:11:09.247 --> 01:11:10.940
but we've gotten to the point now

01:11:10.940 --> 01:11:12.800
where we've just retrained our pallets.

01:11:12.800 --> 01:11:15.000
And it's amazing how this happens now,

01:11:15.000 --> 01:11:18.270
where I'll have something
that is something

01:11:18.270 --> 01:11:20.770
that I would've used to have like daily.

01:11:20.770 --> 01:11:22.840
And it's unpalatable.

01:11:22.840 --> 01:11:24.940
I just can't deal with
the sweetness of it.

01:11:24.940 --> 01:11:28.970
And I certainly avoid
artificial sweeteners,

01:11:28.970 --> 01:11:31.480
but I also avoid just
sweet things in general

01:11:31.480 --> 01:11:34.330
that have sugar in them,
just because they now,

01:11:34.330 --> 01:11:38.550
was originally, I was
trying to be disciplined

01:11:38.550 --> 01:11:39.580
and trying to change my diet,

01:11:39.580 --> 01:11:41.310
but now they just don't taste good to me.

01:11:41.310 --> 01:11:42.143
- Yeah, likewise,

01:11:42.143 --> 01:11:44.800
I completely lost my appetite for sugar

01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:46.020
at the turn of the last year,

01:11:46.020 --> 01:11:48.874
and I don't know how to
explain it, but the way I...

01:11:48.874 --> 01:11:51.650
Even though I don't have
a mechanistic explanation,

01:11:51.650 --> 01:11:54.470
I say I like sweet people, I
don't like sweet food anymore.

01:11:54.470 --> 01:11:55.400
I just don't.

01:11:55.400 --> 01:11:58.340
I have not lost my
appetite for fatty foods.

01:11:58.340 --> 01:12:02.450
I love cheese and certain meats for me,

01:12:02.450 --> 01:12:05.030
I blame my Argentine lineage is,

01:12:05.030 --> 01:12:07.250
is I gravitate towards them.

01:12:07.250 --> 01:12:10.530
But in any case, avoiding processed foods,

01:12:10.530 --> 01:12:12.950
probably avoiding sugars, emulsifiers,

01:12:12.950 --> 01:12:13.783
these kinds of things.

01:12:13.783 --> 01:12:15.850
And for people listening or watching,

01:12:15.850 --> 01:12:17.800
we're not setting up strict guidelines.

01:12:17.800 --> 01:12:21.670
We're just bouncing around the carnival

01:12:21.670 --> 01:12:24.170
that is the microbiome and nutrition,

01:12:24.170 --> 01:12:27.450
because I think that we
hear this everywhere,

01:12:27.450 --> 01:12:29.900
eat this, don't eat that, or
this is best for microbiome,

01:12:29.900 --> 01:12:30.760
or worse for microbiome.

01:12:30.760 --> 01:12:32.640
But I'm hearing fiber again and again,

01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:33.764
so we're going to come back to fiber,

01:12:33.764 --> 01:12:36.750
but I want to make sure
that we close the hatch

01:12:36.750 --> 01:12:38.774
on this issue of fasting and cleansing.

01:12:38.774 --> 01:12:41.690
Based on your answer
earlier, it sounds to me like

01:12:41.690 --> 01:12:45.160
it is not necessary to
do a cleanse or fast

01:12:45.160 --> 01:12:48.660
prior to an attempt to
repopulate the microbiome.

01:12:48.660 --> 01:12:51.270
In other words, if I want to
make my microbiome healthier,

01:12:51.270 --> 01:12:53.350
it sounds like I don't
have to try and flush

01:12:53.350 --> 01:12:56.310
all the current microbiota
out of there first,

01:12:56.310 --> 01:12:57.143
is that correct?

01:12:57.143 --> 01:12:59.240
- Yeah, it's a very good question,

01:12:59.240 --> 01:13:00.544
and I don't mean to
suggest that those things

01:13:00.544 --> 01:13:03.840
are known to be terrible.

01:13:03.840 --> 01:13:06.461
Or I would just say, the
studies haven't been done.

01:13:06.461 --> 01:13:10.448
And to me, wiping out
this microbial community,

01:13:10.448 --> 01:13:13.590
unless it's done with some sort of...

01:13:13.590 --> 01:13:15.300
Unless it's done in an informed way,

01:13:15.300 --> 01:13:16.780
and we don't really have the information

01:13:16.780 --> 01:13:18.100
for how that would be done.

01:13:18.100 --> 01:13:21.740
It just seems like playing
the lottery a little bit.

01:13:21.740 --> 01:13:23.950
- Okay.
- And so I think...

01:13:23.950 --> 01:13:25.020
I don't want to say that those are...

01:13:25.020 --> 01:13:26.870
It may be that when the study is done,

01:13:26.870 --> 01:13:28.530
those are shown to be amazing,

01:13:28.530 --> 01:13:30.690
but I just don't think we have
the data to know that yet.

01:13:30.690 --> 01:13:33.080
So it's somewhat of an arbitrary thing.

01:13:33.080 --> 01:13:37.000
If somebody out there feels
way better when they do this,

01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:39.083
and are not experiencing problems with it,

01:13:39.083 --> 01:13:41.020
then maybe it's the right thing for them.

01:13:41.020 --> 01:13:44.175
But I certainly can't say that
it's something great to do.

01:13:44.175 --> 01:13:48.770
I can't imagine a future
where as the microbiome

01:13:48.770 --> 01:13:51.430
gets incorporated into
this emerging paradigm

01:13:51.430 --> 01:13:54.190
of precision health, you go into a clinic,

01:13:54.190 --> 01:13:56.150
somebody types your microbiome and says,

01:13:56.150 --> 01:13:59.380
oh, there's this huge,
massive misconfiguration.

01:13:59.380 --> 01:14:01.690
You have all these engrafted bacteria

01:14:01.690 --> 01:14:03.583
that are residents in your gut microbiome

01:14:03.583 --> 01:14:06.120
that are sending out molecules

01:14:06.120 --> 01:14:07.730
that are not good for your health.

01:14:07.730 --> 01:14:10.660
It would be good if we do
a mass-reprogramming of it.

01:14:10.660 --> 01:14:13.680
The way that we do that
is we flush your gut,

01:14:13.680 --> 01:14:16.110
and we actually give a
light antibiotic treatment

01:14:16.110 --> 01:14:18.080
to try to kill everything that's there.

01:14:18.080 --> 01:14:21.480
And then we repopulate
with this other consortium

01:14:21.480 --> 01:14:24.160
of microbes that we've
studied and know are healthy,

01:14:24.160 --> 01:14:25.993
know are compatible
with your human genome,

01:14:25.993 --> 01:14:28.136
and can be reinforced with a diet

01:14:28.136 --> 01:14:29.676
that we know is good for you.

01:14:29.676 --> 01:14:31.710
We'll install those microbes,

01:14:31.710 --> 01:14:34.084
we'll help you along
in the diet so you know

01:14:34.084 --> 01:14:36.400
how to nourish those microbes,

01:14:36.400 --> 01:14:38.470
and that will be the way
that will reconfigure

01:14:38.470 --> 01:14:39.410
your gut microbiome.

01:14:39.410 --> 01:14:42.890
So I can't imagine a future
where that sort of flushing,

01:14:42.890 --> 01:14:46.700
or cleansing is part of something
for repopulating the gut.

01:14:46.700 --> 01:14:49.890
But right now it seems a
little half-baked to me.

01:14:49.890 --> 01:14:50.923
Yeah.
- Great.

01:14:51.970 --> 01:14:54.960
I'd love to talk about
fiber and fermented foods,

01:14:54.960 --> 01:14:58.500
because you and Chris had a really,

01:14:58.500 --> 01:15:01.660
what I think is a really
interesting and exciting paper

01:15:01.660 --> 01:15:06.389
at the end of last year about
comparing the inflammatome,

01:15:06.389 --> 01:15:10.190
so inflammatory markers of people who ate

01:15:10.190 --> 01:15:11.310
a certain amount of fiber,

01:15:11.310 --> 01:15:13.653
or a certain amount of
these fermented foods.

01:15:14.590 --> 01:15:16.710
This study is amazing for several reasons,

01:15:16.710 --> 01:15:21.360
but almost as amazing as how diverse

01:15:21.360 --> 01:15:24.690
the interpretation of this
study was in the media.

01:15:24.690 --> 01:15:28.850
If ever there was a study
that was kind of hijacked

01:15:28.850 --> 01:15:31.810
by different priority schemes out there-

01:15:31.810 --> 01:15:33.560
- Yeah.
- It's this study.

01:15:33.560 --> 01:15:36.500
So you performed the study with Chris,

01:15:36.500 --> 01:15:39.350
and your postdocs, and
graduate students and staff.

01:15:39.350 --> 01:15:42.680
What are the major conclusions
and what sorts of directives,

01:15:42.680 --> 01:15:44.570
if any, emerged from this study?

01:15:44.570 --> 01:15:46.820
And I'll just preface
this again by saying,

01:15:46.820 --> 01:15:50.257
if I wasn't clear, some news report said,

01:15:50.257 --> 01:15:52.680
"Ah, this means fiber is not important."

01:15:52.680 --> 01:15:54.145
- Yeah.
- And then others said,

01:15:54.145 --> 01:15:57.490
this means fermented foods
and fiber are important.

01:15:57.490 --> 01:16:00.120
And others said, fermented
foods are the thing,

01:16:00.120 --> 01:16:01.420
and the only thing.

01:16:01.420 --> 01:16:03.414
It was all over the place.
- Yep.

01:16:03.414 --> 01:16:06.990
- And one of the reasons for
doing this podcast at all

01:16:06.990 --> 01:16:08.410
is so that we can go straight

01:16:08.410 --> 01:16:10.728
to the people who perform the work.

01:16:10.728 --> 01:16:11.561
- Fantastic.
- And even though

01:16:11.561 --> 01:16:13.670
I'm certainly not an expert in microbiome,

01:16:13.670 --> 01:16:15.800
to give you the opportunity
to share with me,

01:16:15.800 --> 01:16:17.960
and me to ask the kinds
of questions that have...

01:16:17.960 --> 01:16:19.020
I have zero agenda.

01:16:19.020 --> 01:16:23.453
I do like sauerkraut, I do
drink the occasional kombucha.

01:16:25.270 --> 01:16:29.240
I do like low sugar, not so
sweet forms of fermented foods.

01:16:29.240 --> 01:16:31.580
So I would be delighted if
fermented foods are good for me,

01:16:31.580 --> 01:16:34.550
but I have no stake in the
fermented and food industry.

01:16:34.550 --> 01:16:35.630
- Yeah, absolutely.
- Yeah.

01:16:35.630 --> 01:16:36.693
- Yeah, yeah. Great.

01:16:37.680 --> 01:16:40.170
Yeah, wonderful, and
an important note there

01:16:40.170 --> 01:16:41.520
is the one you pointed out,

01:16:41.520 --> 01:16:43.070
that this is an incredible collaboration

01:16:43.070 --> 01:16:44.370
with Christopher Gardner's lab,

01:16:44.370 --> 01:16:46.840
and a bunch of people.

01:16:46.840 --> 01:16:49.900
Erica Sonnenburg helped lead
this study, and then tons of,

01:16:49.900 --> 01:16:51.500
like you were saying, postdoc staff

01:16:51.500 --> 01:16:53.850
and other people at Stanford,

01:16:53.850 --> 01:16:55.200
and then wonderful participants

01:16:55.200 --> 01:16:57.360
that were part of this study.

01:16:57.360 --> 01:16:59.033
So a huge team effort.

01:16:59.880 --> 01:17:01.700
Let me tell you, before
I dive into that study,

01:17:01.700 --> 01:17:03.890
let me take a step back,
because I think the reason

01:17:03.890 --> 01:17:06.980
that we did this study, and
kind of Christopher's group,

01:17:06.980 --> 01:17:11.270
and our group has started
to pursue this line

01:17:11.270 --> 01:17:13.450
of looking at dietary interventions

01:17:13.450 --> 01:17:14.910
and how they impact our microbiome,

01:17:14.910 --> 01:17:17.203
how they impact human biology,

01:17:17.203 --> 01:17:22.070
goes back to this kind of
epiphany that we we had

01:17:22.070 --> 01:17:23.590
while studying the gut microbiome.

01:17:23.590 --> 01:17:26.540
Because I think when we started
studying it at Stanford,

01:17:26.540 --> 01:17:28.210
we were thinking about it as this

01:17:28.210 --> 01:17:30.900
kind of newly appreciated
aspect of our biology,

01:17:30.900 --> 01:17:34.913
almost like finding an organ
that we didn't know was there,

01:17:34.913 --> 01:17:37.900
and starting to think about
like all the drug targets

01:17:37.900 --> 01:17:38.733
that were there.

01:17:38.733 --> 01:17:40.370
Can we go in with small molecule drugs,

01:17:40.370 --> 01:17:41.912
and think of ways to
manipulate this community

01:17:41.912 --> 01:17:43.801
to ameliorate disease?

01:17:43.801 --> 01:17:47.400
And this is largely the
mindset of Western medicine,

01:17:47.400 --> 01:17:50.350
and largely born out of the
era of infectious disease.

01:17:50.350 --> 01:17:53.610
You wait for an infection to
start a bacterial infection,

01:17:53.610 --> 01:17:54.850
you treat with antibiotics,

01:17:54.850 --> 01:17:56.880
and that's the way medicine is practiced,

01:17:56.880 --> 01:17:59.820
and that's become less
successful over time,

01:17:59.820 --> 01:18:03.373
as we've moved into this
era of inflammatory,

01:18:03.373 --> 01:18:07.530
Western diseases, and with the exception

01:18:07.530 --> 01:18:11.605
of the current pandemic
that's sweeping the world,

01:18:11.605 --> 01:18:15.070
largely moved out of the
era of infectious diseases,

01:18:15.070 --> 01:18:17.290
at least infectious bacterial diseases,

01:18:17.290 --> 01:18:21.230
that this paradigm of waiting
for diseases to appear,

01:18:21.230 --> 01:18:25.050
and come into the clinic is
not really very effective

01:18:25.050 --> 01:18:27.840
in the context of
inflammatory, Western diseases,

01:18:27.840 --> 01:18:29.906
autoimmune diseases, metabolic syndrome,

01:18:29.906 --> 01:18:32.300
heart diseases, and inflammatory disease.

01:18:32.300 --> 01:18:33.920
The list goes on and on.

01:18:33.920 --> 01:18:37.960
And so we started to
think a lot about like,

01:18:37.960 --> 01:18:39.390
how can we get out in front of this?

01:18:39.390 --> 01:18:42.280
How can we think about preventative ways

01:18:42.280 --> 01:18:45.350
of dealing with this crisis of metabolic

01:18:45.350 --> 01:18:46.791
and inflammatory diseases?

01:18:46.791 --> 01:18:50.150
And this tremendous,
beautiful body of literature

01:18:50.150 --> 01:18:53.567
started to come forward in
the field about 10 years ago

01:18:53.567 --> 01:18:57.900
that showed that the gut
microbiome is absolutely critical

01:18:57.900 --> 01:18:59.770
to modulating our immune status.

01:18:59.770 --> 01:19:01.750
So if you change the microbiome,

01:19:01.750 --> 01:19:05.470
you can fundamentally change
how the immune system operates.

01:19:05.470 --> 01:19:07.270
And we know that the immune system

01:19:07.270 --> 01:19:11.240
is at the basis of a lot of these disease,

01:19:11.240 --> 01:19:13.480
inflammatory, chronic diseases.

01:19:13.480 --> 01:19:16.820
And so it brought up this
possibility that maybe the fact

01:19:16.820 --> 01:19:19.760
that we're not nourishing
this community well enough,

01:19:19.760 --> 01:19:22.970
maybe the fact that it's
deteriorated over time

01:19:23.823 --> 01:19:25.520
due to all of the things that go along

01:19:25.520 --> 01:19:29.760
with an industrialized lifestyle,
antibiotics and so forth.

01:19:29.760 --> 01:19:32.660
Maybe we have a microbiome right now

01:19:32.660 --> 01:19:34.950
in the industrialized
world that is setting

01:19:34.950 --> 01:19:38.340
our immune system at a set
point, simmering inflammation

01:19:38.340 --> 01:19:41.490
that's driving us towards
these inflammatory diseases.

01:19:41.490 --> 01:19:44.480
And wouldn't it be wonderful
if we could figure out how to

01:19:46.730 --> 01:19:50.270
use diet specifically, but
just kind of learn the rules

01:19:50.270 --> 01:19:52.990
of how to reconfigure both the composition

01:19:52.990 --> 01:19:54.840
and function of our gut microbiome,

01:19:54.840 --> 01:19:58.350
so that inflammation was
different in our bodies,

01:19:58.350 --> 01:20:00.618
so that each one of us was less likely

01:20:00.618 --> 01:20:03.300
to go on and to develop
an inflammatory disease,

01:20:03.300 --> 01:20:06.429
leading to better longevity and health

01:20:06.429 --> 01:20:08.410
over the course of our life.

01:20:08.410 --> 01:20:13.410
And so we were studying this
in actually in mouse models,

01:20:14.420 --> 01:20:17.550
and realizing that we really needed

01:20:17.550 --> 01:20:19.040
to start doing human studies.

01:20:19.040 --> 01:20:23.290
We needed to start studying
microbiome in humans,

01:20:23.290 --> 01:20:25.187
and because we were studying diet,

01:20:25.187 --> 01:20:27.340
we knew that this was
something we could go in

01:20:27.340 --> 01:20:28.173
and do right away.

01:20:28.173 --> 01:20:30.890
We didn't have to apply
for FDA approval for a drug

01:20:30.890 --> 01:20:32.490
before we could do a human study,

01:20:32.490 --> 01:20:34.866
we could just start doing
human dietary interventions,

01:20:34.866 --> 01:20:38.206
longitudinally monitoring
the immune system,

01:20:38.206 --> 01:20:41.990
and the microbiome, and starting
to put the pieces together

01:20:41.990 --> 01:20:43.790
of what is it in diet that can change

01:20:43.790 --> 01:20:45.500
our microbiome in a healthy way,

01:20:45.500 --> 01:20:48.260
help us define what a
healthy microbiome is,

01:20:48.260 --> 01:20:51.340
and monitor the immune
system in great detail.

01:20:51.340 --> 01:20:53.830
And so there were really two
critical components of this,

01:20:53.830 --> 01:20:56.110
in addition to our microbiome expertise.

01:20:56.110 --> 01:20:58.035
One was Christopher Gardner's group.

01:20:58.035 --> 01:21:00.540
We wanted to do these human studies,

01:21:00.540 --> 01:21:03.080
but we're absolutely terrified of humans.

01:21:03.080 --> 01:21:06.750
We work with mice. Humans
are terrifying in many ways.

01:21:06.750 --> 01:21:08.470
- But they house themselves,
you don't have to pay-

01:21:08.470 --> 01:21:09.303
- That's true.
- You don't have to pay

01:21:09.303 --> 01:21:10.900
for their housing.
- That's true.

01:21:10.900 --> 01:21:13.484
- For those that can afford
housing, of course, yeah.

01:21:13.484 --> 01:21:16.623
- Yeah, sadly, just for that
portion of the population.

01:21:18.190 --> 01:21:19.270
So Christopher's group were,

01:21:19.270 --> 01:21:21.970
they were our masters at
working with human populations.

01:21:21.970 --> 01:21:24.470
And then the other wonderful
thing that we have at Stanford

01:21:24.470 --> 01:21:26.106
is this Human Immune Monitoring Center

01:21:26.106 --> 01:21:28.690
run by Mark Davis and Holden Maecker.

01:21:28.690 --> 01:21:32.700
They started this beautiful
center for allowing people

01:21:32.700 --> 01:21:35.370
to do immunology in humans.

01:21:35.370 --> 01:21:37.780
Critical element, because
a lot of the mouse studies

01:21:37.780 --> 01:21:39.180
don't translate well to humans.

01:21:39.180 --> 01:21:40.634
So if you can do the studies in humans,

01:21:40.634 --> 01:21:43.185
similar to how we were
thinking about the microbiome,

01:21:43.185 --> 01:21:45.970
you learn something that you
know is relevant to humans.

01:21:45.970 --> 01:21:49.050
And so having that immune
profiling capability

01:21:49.050 --> 01:21:51.797
where we can monitor hundreds to thousands

01:21:51.797 --> 01:21:54.450
of different parameters
in the immune system,

01:21:54.450 --> 01:21:57.090
longitudinally in people,
from a blood draw,

01:21:57.090 --> 01:22:00.550
and not just know if CRP
goes up or if interleukin-6

01:22:00.550 --> 01:22:03.430
goes up or down, but to be
able to see all these facets

01:22:03.430 --> 01:22:05.320
of the immune system change in concert,

01:22:05.320 --> 01:22:07.500
as we're changing the microbiome with diet

01:22:07.500 --> 01:22:09.450
was really a key component of this.

01:22:09.450 --> 01:22:13.726
And so our flagship study,
supported by wonderful donors.

01:22:13.726 --> 01:22:17.350
So this actually isn't
funded by typical foundations

01:22:17.350 --> 01:22:18.590
and national institutes of health,

01:22:18.590 --> 01:22:21.480
it was funded by philanthropy.

01:22:21.480 --> 01:22:24.040
We wanted to understand if we put people

01:22:24.040 --> 01:22:26.040
on a high fiber diet,

01:22:26.040 --> 01:22:28.270
how would that affect their
microbiome immune system?

01:22:28.270 --> 01:22:30.770
And if we put them on a high food diet,

01:22:30.770 --> 01:22:33.660
a diet rich in live microbes
and all the metabolites

01:22:33.660 --> 01:22:36.360
that are present from
fermentation and foods,

01:22:36.360 --> 01:22:38.540
how would that change
microbiome in immune system?

01:22:38.540 --> 01:22:39.660
- Could you give us some examples

01:22:39.660 --> 01:22:40.760
of what those diets look like?

01:22:40.760 --> 01:22:42.551
And were you changing their basal diet,

01:22:42.551 --> 01:22:44.530
or were you just adding things on top

01:22:44.530 --> 01:22:46.876
of what they were already eating?

01:22:46.876 --> 01:22:49.390
'Cause it's hard to change people's diets.

01:22:49.390 --> 01:22:50.560
- It's very hard.
- And then you have to trust

01:22:50.560 --> 01:22:54.030
that they actually do it and
they're not sneaking, and-

01:22:54.030 --> 01:22:55.153
- Totally, yeah.

01:22:56.979 --> 01:22:59.500
We started this center for
human microbiome studies

01:22:59.500 --> 01:23:01.274
at Stanford for doing
a lot of these studies,

01:23:01.274 --> 01:23:04.740
and a portion of the studies,
we do focus on supplements,

01:23:04.740 --> 01:23:07.940
probiotics, microbes
delivered in pill form.

01:23:07.940 --> 01:23:11.870
Prebiotics, which are
purified forms of fiber.

01:23:11.870 --> 01:23:16.590
And in those cases, we actually
can have placebo groups,

01:23:16.590 --> 01:23:19.340
because it's more like a drug study,

01:23:19.340 --> 01:23:22.130
and we don't change people's diets.

01:23:22.130 --> 01:23:24.890
So we can just administer this
on top of what they're doing.

01:23:24.890 --> 01:23:26.770
So in a way, they're
a lot more controlled,

01:23:26.770 --> 01:23:28.310
but it's not food.

01:23:28.310 --> 01:23:29.950
When you start doing food studies,

01:23:29.950 --> 01:23:31.490
you can't do a placebo group,

01:23:31.490 --> 01:23:33.030
'cause people know what they're eating.

01:23:33.030 --> 01:23:35.280
And the other problem is that

01:23:36.297 --> 01:23:39.410
it's really hard to just change one thing,

01:23:39.410 --> 01:23:41.710
because as soon as you
start adding something,

01:23:41.710 --> 01:23:43.530
people usually eliminate something else.

01:23:43.530 --> 01:23:46.840
So the idea was to
basically give these people

01:23:46.840 --> 01:23:50.660
simple instructions for in the
case of the high fiber diet,

01:23:50.660 --> 01:23:53.370
just increasing plant-based fiber.

01:23:53.370 --> 01:23:55.780
So can you eat more whole
grains, more legumes,

01:23:55.780 --> 01:24:00.150
more vegetables, nuts, get
the fiber up in the range of,

01:24:00.150 --> 01:24:04.380
from 15 to 20 grams per day,
up to over 40 grams per day.

01:24:04.380 --> 01:24:07.140
So can you double or more the amount

01:24:07.140 --> 01:24:08.768
of fiber that you eat per day,

01:24:08.768 --> 01:24:12.300
knowing that that would
have a tremendous impact

01:24:12.300 --> 01:24:15.080
on a lot of other facets of their diet.

01:24:15.080 --> 01:24:20.080
They eat less meat, animal-based protein,

01:24:20.177 --> 01:24:23.113
less animal-based fats
as a product of this.

01:24:23.113 --> 01:24:27.580
I will say that getting
back to Christopher's rule

01:24:27.580 --> 01:24:31.910
for a healthy diet, a lot of
the macro nutrient changes

01:24:31.910 --> 01:24:35.210
that we saw in their diet were consistent

01:24:35.210 --> 01:24:37.990
with healthy changes in diet.

01:24:37.990 --> 01:24:41.170
Less saturated fat, less
animal-based protein,

01:24:41.170 --> 01:24:42.590
more plant-based protein.

01:24:42.590 --> 01:24:45.550
So a lot of changes that are known

01:24:45.550 --> 01:24:48.670
to be beneficial came in concert
with just telling people,

01:24:48.670 --> 01:24:53.020
eat a high fiber diet, high
plant-based fiber diet.

01:24:53.020 --> 01:24:57.383
The people that were eating
the high fermented food diet,

01:24:58.370 --> 01:25:00.920
they were instructed to basically eat

01:25:02.151 --> 01:25:04.760
foods that you could
buy at a grocery store

01:25:04.760 --> 01:25:07.950
that were naturally fermented
and contain live microbes.

01:25:07.950 --> 01:25:12.950
And so this largely
consisted of yogurt, kefir,

01:25:13.520 --> 01:25:18.520
sauerkraut, kimchi, some
fermented vegetables,

01:25:18.780 --> 01:25:20.080
brined, fermented vegetables.

01:25:20.080 --> 01:25:22.133
- Pickles.
- Pickles, things like that.

01:25:23.780 --> 01:25:25.890
One of the things that
I think is a pitfall

01:25:25.890 --> 01:25:27.447
in choosing fermented foods is,

01:25:27.447 --> 01:25:29.850
you can go down the canned food aisle

01:25:29.850 --> 01:25:32.430
and there's this huge section of pickles,

01:25:32.430 --> 01:25:34.450
and jars that are canned.

01:25:34.450 --> 01:25:36.320
Those are not fermented foods.

01:25:36.320 --> 01:25:39.150
Those are are cucumbers that
they've put in a acidic acid

01:25:39.150 --> 01:25:43.100
and vinegar to reconstitute
that fermented flavor,

01:25:43.100 --> 01:25:45.700
but there's no live
microbes involved in that.

01:25:45.700 --> 01:25:47.940
And even sauerkrauts in
the canned food aisle,

01:25:47.940 --> 01:25:49.760
even if they were naturally fermented,

01:25:49.760 --> 01:25:50.620
quite often they're not.

01:25:50.620 --> 01:25:54.150
Quite often they're
just brined in vinegar.

01:25:54.150 --> 01:25:56.020
But even if they are naturally fermented,

01:25:56.020 --> 01:25:58.830
all the microbes are
killed prior to canning

01:25:58.830 --> 01:26:00.480
or during the process of canning.

01:26:01.394 --> 01:26:02.820
So what we use for this study

01:26:02.820 --> 01:26:04.700
and if you want to have
live fermented foods

01:26:04.700 --> 01:26:06.870
that contain live microbes,
you need to buy those

01:26:06.870 --> 01:26:10.780
out of the refrigerated
section, essentially.

01:26:10.780 --> 01:26:12.938
- And I'm really glad
you pointed this out,

01:26:12.938 --> 01:26:15.300
because you can find sauerkraut

01:26:15.300 --> 01:26:20.300
on the non-refrigerated shelf,
that is indeed non fermented.

01:26:21.000 --> 01:26:23.840
A lot of fermented that
are available in the US

01:26:24.810 --> 01:26:26.770
can be high in sugar.

01:26:26.770 --> 01:26:30.020
So was there any instruction
as to getting people

01:26:30.020 --> 01:26:32.350
to make sure that they
were consuming yogurts

01:26:32.350 --> 01:26:34.450
that weren't loaded with sugar?

01:26:34.450 --> 01:26:35.970
- Yep.
- Or did you let them just

01:26:35.970 --> 01:26:40.956
select for the stuff in the
cold section that is fermented?

01:26:40.956 --> 01:26:42.379
- No, super important point.

01:26:42.379 --> 01:26:46.143
We instructed people to
eat non-sweetened yogurts.

01:26:47.123 --> 01:26:51.190
I think a huge pitfall in this
area is you can have a yogurt

01:26:51.190 --> 01:26:54.650
loaded with bacteria, kind of
the base of what's healthy,

01:26:54.650 --> 01:26:57.350
and then a ton of like
artificial flavoring

01:26:57.350 --> 01:26:59.020
and sugar loaded on top of that.

01:26:59.020 --> 01:27:01.920
Manufacturers put a ton
of sugar in after the fact

01:27:01.920 --> 01:27:04.470
to kind of mask the sour
taste fermented foods,

01:27:04.470 --> 01:27:07.410
which is hard for some people
to become accustomed to.

01:27:07.410 --> 01:27:09.910
When we were switching
to more fermented foods,

01:27:09.910 --> 01:27:11.670
our daughters were young at that point,

01:27:11.670 --> 01:27:13.610
we would take plain yogurt,

01:27:13.610 --> 01:27:17.080
which they didn't like just neat,

01:27:17.080 --> 01:27:20.120
we would mix in a little
maple syrup or honey,

01:27:20.120 --> 01:27:22.680
just a little bit, and
gradually we would reduced that

01:27:22.680 --> 01:27:25.640
over time to the point where
they're pallet adjusted,

01:27:25.640 --> 01:27:28.620
and now they just really
like plain yogurt.

01:27:28.620 --> 01:27:32.201
But it is, I think, getting
used to that sour flavor

01:27:32.201 --> 01:27:35.100
is difficult, but people
really should try to stay away

01:27:35.100 --> 01:27:37.810
from those fermented foods
that are loaded with sugar.

01:27:37.810 --> 01:27:40.140
And that's what we instructed
people in this study.

01:27:40.140 --> 01:27:41.870
- And beer was not included.

01:27:41.870 --> 01:27:43.190
- Right.
- The number of people

01:27:43.190 --> 01:27:46.170
that asked, when I did a
brief thing on social media

01:27:46.170 --> 01:27:48.100
about this study, and
hopefully I got it right.

01:27:48.100 --> 01:27:49.690
I think I did.

01:27:49.690 --> 01:27:51.190
But people just ask about beer.

01:27:51.190 --> 01:27:56.060
I'm not a drinker, so for me,
beer has no appeal anyway.

01:27:56.060 --> 01:28:00.090
But beer is fermented, correct?

01:28:00.090 --> 01:28:03.160
But were they instructed to
avoid beer or to drink beer?

01:28:03.160 --> 01:28:05.320
- Just to go with their
normal dietary habits,

01:28:05.320 --> 01:28:07.850
but that did not count
as a fermented food.

01:28:07.850 --> 01:28:09.290
- And kombucha was, as I recall-

01:28:09.290 --> 01:28:10.780
- Kombucha was, and kombucha can have

01:28:10.780 --> 01:28:12.393
small amounts of alcohol in it.

01:28:15.850 --> 01:28:17.813
Yeah, kombucha actually
was one of the major things

01:28:17.813 --> 01:28:19.850
that people drank during,

01:28:19.850 --> 01:28:22.420
or consumed during the
fermented food phase.

01:28:22.420 --> 01:28:27.060
And the deal with beer

01:28:27.060 --> 01:28:30.690
is that there may be beneficial properties

01:28:30.690 --> 01:28:33.160
of the microbial communities
in naturally fermented beer,

01:28:33.160 --> 01:28:34.730
but most of the beer that we buy, again,

01:28:34.730 --> 01:28:38.130
is canned and filtered, and
there's no live microbes there.

01:28:38.130 --> 01:28:40.840
So very different than
if you siphon it off

01:28:40.840 --> 01:28:42.860
of your home brew and drink it, probably,

01:28:42.860 --> 01:28:44.540
than if you buy it in a store.

01:28:44.540 --> 01:28:47.370
- I will get to the results of
the study in just one moment.

01:28:47.370 --> 01:28:49.640
But I want to say, a
lot of people shy away

01:28:49.640 --> 01:28:51.990
from the high quality fermented foods,

01:28:51.990 --> 01:28:54.010
because they can be quite costly.

01:28:54.010 --> 01:28:56.300
And I'll just refer people to a resource

01:28:56.300 --> 01:28:58.740
in Tim Ferriss's book, "The 4-Hour Chef,"

01:28:58.740 --> 01:29:00.390
he actually gives an excellent recipe

01:29:00.390 --> 01:29:01.650
for making your own sauerkraut,

01:29:01.650 --> 01:29:06.580
which basically involves
cabbage and water and salt,

01:29:06.580 --> 01:29:08.030
but you have to do it properly,

01:29:08.030 --> 01:29:11.670
because you can grow some,
not necessarily lethal,

01:29:11.670 --> 01:29:13.960
but some somewhat dangerous bacteria

01:29:13.960 --> 01:29:16.070
if you don't scrape off
the top layer properly,

01:29:16.070 --> 01:29:17.480
but he gives beautiful instructions

01:29:17.480 --> 01:29:18.890
for how to do this in vats.

01:29:18.890 --> 01:29:21.080
We've started doing this
at home now, actually,

01:29:21.080 --> 01:29:22.750
is we got a, just ceramic vat.

01:29:22.750 --> 01:29:26.660
And you can make large amounts
of truly fermented sauerkraut

01:29:26.660 --> 01:29:28.930
just from cabbage water and salt,

01:29:28.930 --> 01:29:30.518
if you're willing to follow the protocol.

01:29:30.518 --> 01:29:32.070
And if you're interested in science,

01:29:32.070 --> 01:29:34.020
that protocol looks a
lot like what you'll do

01:29:34.020 --> 01:29:35.880
for most of your graduate career,

01:29:35.880 --> 01:29:37.840
except maybe some sequencing too.

01:29:37.840 --> 01:29:39.710
So anyway, just to
refer people to a source

01:29:39.710 --> 01:29:41.340
that's very low cost.

01:29:41.340 --> 01:29:42.550
- Yeah.
- Compared to buying

01:29:42.550 --> 01:29:43.730
the high quality, fermented foods.

01:29:43.730 --> 01:29:47.410
Even kombuchas, for some
people, it's like $5 a bottle-

01:29:47.410 --> 01:29:48.630
- Totally.
- Only this much.

01:29:48.630 --> 01:29:50.710
And if you consume liquids
the way I consume them,

01:29:50.710 --> 01:29:53.315
that's just the the start, so-

01:29:53.315 --> 01:29:54.830
- Yeah.

01:29:54.830 --> 01:29:56.940
But if you can get your hands on a SCOBY,

01:29:56.940 --> 01:29:59.120
kombucha is another one
that's super simple.

01:29:59.120 --> 01:29:59.953
- You can grow your own.

01:29:59.953 --> 01:30:01.060
- You can just make your own,

01:30:01.060 --> 01:30:02.510
and it's super easy to do.

01:30:02.510 --> 01:30:04.650
I make it, I constantly have a batch

01:30:04.650 --> 01:30:06.023
of kombucha going at home.

01:30:07.286 --> 01:30:10.703
It's a SCOBY, a symbiotic
community of bacteria and yeast.

01:30:12.007 --> 01:30:15.580
You brew tea, you add sugar to
it, and you put the SCOBY in,

01:30:15.580 --> 01:30:18.240
and you wait a week or two,
depending upon the temperature,

01:30:18.240 --> 01:30:22.870
and then you just move the
SCOBY or over to a new batch,

01:30:22.870 --> 01:30:25.560
and you your old, what the
SCOBY was in is kombucha,

01:30:25.560 --> 01:30:26.570
and it's wonderful.

01:30:26.570 --> 01:30:28.670
- I love it, I would love it
if members of this audience

01:30:28.670 --> 01:30:31.416
would start to make their
own kombucha and sauerkraut.

01:30:31.416 --> 01:30:33.250
I've been having so much fun.

01:30:33.250 --> 01:30:37.320
I don't do it, but it's done in our home.

01:30:37.320 --> 01:30:39.047
I don't go anywhere near
the food production,

01:30:39.047 --> 01:30:41.163
and it's for everyone's benefit.

01:30:42.220 --> 01:30:46.340
So how much fermented
food were they consuming?

01:30:46.340 --> 01:30:47.520
'Cause you mentioned the number

01:30:47.520 --> 01:30:50.649
of grams approximately of fiber,

01:30:50.649 --> 01:30:54.200
but was it in servings, ounces?

01:30:54.200 --> 01:30:56.450
How many times a day? Early day, late day?

01:30:56.450 --> 01:30:57.283
- Right, yeah.

01:30:57.283 --> 01:31:02.120
So we had a wonderful dietician
instructing people for this,

01:31:02.120 --> 01:31:03.670
and her name's Dalia Perelman,

01:31:03.670 --> 01:31:05.470
and she really was the key and is the key

01:31:05.470 --> 01:31:06.303
for many of our studies

01:31:06.303 --> 01:31:08.190
for getting people to eat differently.

01:31:08.190 --> 01:31:12.090
And the general instructions
were for people to eat

01:31:12.090 --> 01:31:14.810
as much fermented foods as
possible, more is better.

01:31:14.810 --> 01:31:18.080
And the reason is that
with this initial study,

01:31:18.080 --> 01:31:20.310
we really wanted to maximize our chance

01:31:20.310 --> 01:31:21.940
of seeing a signal if there was

01:31:21.940 --> 01:31:23.500
something biological going on,

01:31:23.500 --> 01:31:27.175
with the idea that if
the dose was excessive

01:31:27.175 --> 01:31:30.270
and not easily achievable by
a lot of people in the end,

01:31:30.270 --> 01:31:32.360
we can go back and and say, okay,

01:31:32.360 --> 01:31:34.877
this is the point at which we
lose the biological signal.

01:31:34.877 --> 01:31:38.660
But people during the height
of the intervention phase,

01:31:38.660 --> 01:31:40.810
the intervention phase was six weeks,

01:31:40.810 --> 01:31:41.887
during the height of that,

01:31:41.887 --> 01:31:44.780
were up over six servings, on average,

01:31:44.780 --> 01:31:46.270
per day of fermented foods.

01:31:46.270 --> 01:31:48.620
So kind of two servings at each meal.

01:31:48.620 --> 01:31:52.159
And the ounces or weight or size,

01:31:52.159 --> 01:31:55.360
it really depended on what
the fermented food was.

01:31:55.360 --> 01:31:56.670
And we just told them to stick

01:31:56.670 --> 01:31:59.820
to what was a recommended
dose on the package

01:31:59.820 --> 01:32:01.920
that they were buying,

01:32:01.920 --> 01:32:04.836
For kombucha, it'd be like
a six to eight ounce glass.

01:32:04.836 --> 01:32:07.337
Sauerkraut like a half cup,
or something like that,

01:32:07.337 --> 01:32:08.720
and same with yogurt.

01:32:08.720 --> 01:32:12.540
- Great. So what were the results?

01:32:12.540 --> 01:32:17.170
- Yeah, so the results
astounded us in a way,

01:32:17.170 --> 01:32:19.210
but then thinking more deeply,

01:32:19.210 --> 01:32:21.846
and it'll be evident
even after I explain it

01:32:21.846 --> 01:32:24.660
in the context of this conversation,

01:32:24.660 --> 01:32:27.720
likely why we saw the results we saw.

01:32:27.720 --> 01:32:31.360
The results were astounding
because our hypothesis

01:32:31.360 --> 01:32:33.940
going into this was
that the high fiber diet

01:32:33.940 --> 01:32:35.600
was going to give the massive signal.

01:32:35.600 --> 01:32:38.600
We know that this is the big
deficiency in the Western diet.

01:32:40.000 --> 01:32:42.500
All the mouse studies have
told us that high fiber

01:32:42.500 --> 01:32:44.060
really leads to a much
healthier microbiota,

01:32:44.060 --> 01:32:47.090
it can lead to positive
changes in the immune system.

01:32:47.090 --> 01:32:49.180
And in fact, even when we had a limited...

01:32:49.180 --> 01:32:50.460
We had wonderful donor support,

01:32:50.460 --> 01:32:51.770
but still a limited amount of money

01:32:51.770 --> 01:32:53.760
when we started this study.

01:32:53.760 --> 01:32:57.829
My lab was really very
eager to do the high fiber

01:32:57.829 --> 01:33:00.280
part of this really well,

01:33:00.280 --> 01:33:02.570
and Christopher kind of
had to twist our arms

01:33:02.570 --> 01:33:04.640
to do the fermented food side of it.

01:33:04.640 --> 01:33:07.590
And we thought it was
kind of quirky and neat,

01:33:07.590 --> 01:33:10.890
live microbes should be
exciting, let's try it.

01:33:10.890 --> 01:33:12.920
So we put that in and it turns out

01:33:12.920 --> 01:33:15.540
that we were very thankful
that he twisted our arms,

01:33:15.540 --> 01:33:18.450
because it was that
high fermented food arm

01:33:18.450 --> 01:33:20.120
that really gave us the big signal.

01:33:20.120 --> 01:33:22.890
Even though our hypothesis
was that the high fiber

01:33:22.890 --> 01:33:24.380
was going to lead to more short-chain

01:33:24.380 --> 01:33:28.814
fatty acids produced in the
gut, more diverse microbiota,

01:33:28.814 --> 01:33:31.410
less inflammation in the immune system,

01:33:31.410 --> 01:33:33.510
we didn't see that across the cohort.

01:33:33.510 --> 01:33:37.050
We actually saw very
individualized responses

01:33:37.050 --> 01:33:38.510
to the dietary fiber,

01:33:38.510 --> 01:33:40.780
and I'll come back to
what those responses were.

01:33:40.780 --> 01:33:44.200
The big signal really was
in the fermented food group.

01:33:44.200 --> 01:33:46.880
We saw all the things
that you would hope to see

01:33:46.880 --> 01:33:49.820
in a Western microbiota in western human.

01:33:49.820 --> 01:33:52.400
We saw this increase
in microbiota diversity

01:33:52.400 --> 01:33:53.700
over the course of the six weeks

01:33:53.700 --> 01:33:55.665
while they were consuming
the fermented foods.

01:33:55.665 --> 01:34:00.665
And we can't always say that
higher diversity is better

01:34:01.320 --> 01:34:03.340
when it comes to our
microbial communities.

01:34:03.340 --> 01:34:06.240
We know there are cases for
instance, bacterial vaginosis,

01:34:06.240 --> 01:34:07.730
where higher diversity is actually

01:34:07.730 --> 01:34:10.330
indicative of a disease state,

01:34:10.330 --> 01:34:13.630
but we know in the context of the gut,

01:34:13.630 --> 01:34:16.430
and for people living in
the industrialized world,

01:34:16.430 --> 01:34:19.310
higher diversity is generally better.

01:34:19.310 --> 01:34:21.430
We know that there's a
spectrum of diversity.

01:34:21.430 --> 01:34:24.540
People with higher diversity
generally are healthier.

01:34:24.540 --> 01:34:26.200
If you can push your diversity higher,

01:34:26.200 --> 01:34:27.340
you're in better shape.

01:34:27.340 --> 01:34:29.640
And so we saw that increase in diversity.

01:34:29.640 --> 01:34:30.850
And then the major question is,

01:34:30.850 --> 01:34:32.340
what happened to the immune system

01:34:32.340 --> 01:34:34.290
as these people were increasing

01:34:34.290 --> 01:34:37.866
their gut microbiota diversity
through the fermented foods?

01:34:37.866 --> 01:34:41.500
So we did this massive immune profiling,

01:34:41.500 --> 01:34:45.060
and we see a couple dozen immune markers,

01:34:45.060 --> 01:34:48.970
inflammatory markers decrease
over the course of the study.

01:34:48.970 --> 01:34:51.970
So we measured these
at multiple time points

01:34:51.970 --> 01:34:52.950
throughout the course of the study,

01:34:52.950 --> 01:34:55.800
and there's kind of this
set stepwise reduction

01:34:55.800 --> 01:35:00.180
in things like interleukin-6,
and interleukin-12,

01:35:00.180 --> 01:35:03.790
a variety of famous
inflammatory mediators.

01:35:03.790 --> 01:35:06.280
And then even if you go
into the immune cells

01:35:06.280 --> 01:35:08.770
and you start looking at
their signaling cascades,

01:35:08.770 --> 01:35:12.120
we see that those signaling
cascades are less activated

01:35:12.120 --> 01:35:13.440
at the end of the study compared

01:35:13.440 --> 01:35:14.830
to the beginning of the study,

01:35:14.830 --> 01:35:17.074
indicating in attenuation of inflammation.

01:35:17.074 --> 01:35:20.670
So kind of exactly what
we would hypothesize

01:35:20.670 --> 01:35:22.470
would lead to less propensity

01:35:22.470 --> 01:35:24.780
for inflammatory disease over time.

01:35:24.780 --> 01:35:28.370
That's a huge extension
of a very short study and-

01:35:28.370 --> 01:35:29.470
- How long was this study again?

01:35:29.470 --> 01:35:33.970
- So the complete protocol
I think was 14 to 17 weeks,

01:35:33.970 --> 01:35:35.090
or something like that.

01:35:35.090 --> 01:35:38.890
The actual intervention phase
consisted of a four week ramp

01:35:38.890 --> 01:35:41.330
and then a six week maintenance period.

01:35:41.330 --> 01:35:43.230
So the intervention itself was 10 weeks,

01:35:43.230 --> 01:35:45.760
but there were six weeks
of really kind of hardcore,

01:35:45.760 --> 01:35:48.810
high levels of fiber or fermented foods.

01:35:48.810 --> 01:35:51.230
- Yeah, and I'm glad
you mentioned the ramp,

01:35:51.230 --> 01:35:54.880
'cause my experience with
fermented foods is that you,

01:35:54.880 --> 01:35:56.600
it can be beneficial to give the system

01:35:56.600 --> 01:35:57.780
an opportunity to act acclimate.

01:35:57.780 --> 01:36:01.100
I mean, if you consume a
giant bowl of sauerkraut,

01:36:01.100 --> 01:36:03.100
it's not going to be the worst
day and night of your life,

01:36:03.100 --> 01:36:04.833
but you'll know you did.

01:36:04.833 --> 01:36:06.520
- Totally.
- We'll just leave it at that.

01:36:06.520 --> 01:36:08.700
And so you want to kind
of acclimate to it.

01:36:08.700 --> 01:36:10.564
- Absolutely.
- I'm at the point now where,

01:36:10.564 --> 01:36:12.250
some people might think this is gross,

01:36:12.250 --> 01:36:15.150
but after I exercise,
I've been sweating a lot,

01:36:15.150 --> 01:36:16.660
I like the saltiness of the...

01:36:16.660 --> 01:36:20.100
I actually drink the
liquid that the sauerkraut

01:36:20.100 --> 01:36:22.990
has been stewing in, and I get,

01:36:22.990 --> 01:36:26.780
I like to think that I consume
some fermentation that way,

01:36:26.780 --> 01:36:30.880
it's salty, it as kind of a
post-training replenishment.

01:36:30.880 --> 01:36:34.450
But if I had done that six
months ago, straight off,

01:36:34.450 --> 01:36:36.330
I think it would've been
pretty rough on my system.

01:36:36.330 --> 01:36:37.930
I started taking little bits of it

01:36:37.930 --> 01:36:39.090
and then adding it each day.

01:36:39.090 --> 01:36:40.687
- Totally, and so both
with the fermented foods

01:36:40.687 --> 01:36:42.440
and the fiber, it's well known

01:36:42.440 --> 01:36:44.420
that this kind of gradual ramping

01:36:44.420 --> 01:36:48.100
is a really important way
of mitigating bloating,

01:36:48.100 --> 01:36:51.010
and other digestive
discomfort that can happen

01:36:51.010 --> 01:36:52.700
when your microbiome reconfigures

01:36:52.700 --> 01:36:55.590
and starts fermenting more,
and changing community members.

01:36:55.590 --> 01:36:58.650
So you should take that
ramp at your own pace.

01:36:58.650 --> 01:37:00.220
If something seems to be going wrong,

01:37:00.220 --> 01:37:02.090
just kind of level off, stay there.

01:37:02.090 --> 01:37:03.500
We did this in a very delicate way

01:37:03.500 --> 01:37:05.130
to get people up to the high dose.

01:37:05.130 --> 01:37:09.919
The brine, just a tangent
here for a second,

01:37:09.919 --> 01:37:12.015
that was actually one of the products

01:37:12.015 --> 01:37:16.373
that we had people use in
the fermented food phase.

01:37:16.373 --> 01:37:18.580
There's actually a
product called Gut Shots,

01:37:18.580 --> 01:37:21.275
which is just bring that they've marketed.

01:37:21.275 --> 01:37:24.430
We actually are now
studying it in the lab.

01:37:24.430 --> 01:37:27.290
I just actually, before
this came from a lab meeting

01:37:27.290 --> 01:37:31.260
where a GI fellow in my lab
is actually putting Gut Shots,

01:37:31.260 --> 01:37:35.627
sterilized gut microbe, or the
fermentation microbes removed

01:37:38.770 --> 01:37:42.090
or present into mice
and looking at changes

01:37:42.090 --> 01:37:44.320
in their mucosal immune system.

01:37:44.320 --> 01:37:46.100
So we're studying this in detail now,

01:37:46.100 --> 01:37:48.516
because it's a rich source of lactate

01:37:48.516 --> 01:37:51.110
and a bunch of other
interesting metabolites.

01:37:51.110 --> 01:37:52.460
- I love that my weird behavior

01:37:52.460 --> 01:37:56.510
is inadvertently being
studied at Stanford Medicine.

01:37:56.510 --> 01:37:59.380
I want to just mention
something about the Gut Shots.

01:37:59.380 --> 01:38:02.120
Those are sold as a drink.

01:38:02.120 --> 01:38:04.530
Those also, just for certain
listeners in different budgets,

01:38:04.530 --> 01:38:06.740
they can be very expensive
if you really think about...

01:38:06.740 --> 01:38:08.760
Some of them are exceedingly expensive.

01:38:08.760 --> 01:38:12.207
But what I described before
with making your own kombuchas,

01:38:13.170 --> 01:38:16.361
it's not quite brining, but
the homemade sour sauerkraut,

01:38:16.361 --> 01:38:18.500
that protocol is out there, as I mentioned

01:38:18.500 --> 01:38:20.870
in Tim's book, "The 4-Hour Chef."

01:38:20.870 --> 01:38:23.570
And you get a lot of
the brining from that,

01:38:23.570 --> 01:38:25.230
an almost endless amount.

01:38:25.230 --> 01:38:28.480
A cautionary note, I once
went into the refrigerator

01:38:28.480 --> 01:38:31.310
and saw something similar to
Gut Shot, it wasn't Gut Shot.

01:38:31.310 --> 01:38:33.860
And I drank the whole 12 ounce bottle,

01:38:33.860 --> 01:38:36.190
and realized that it was 24 servings.

01:38:36.190 --> 01:38:39.340
And that's where I got
my initial experience

01:38:39.340 --> 01:38:41.340
with what it is to not do a ramp up phase.

01:38:41.340 --> 01:38:42.470
- Yeah.
I do not recommend

01:38:42.470 --> 01:38:43.303
doing that.

01:38:43.303 --> 01:38:45.370
Some of these, it's very potent, it seems,

01:38:45.370 --> 01:38:47.842
and you can consume even a
half an ounce or an ounce.

01:38:47.842 --> 01:38:50.410
- Yeah, I mean, very
potent from the standpoint

01:38:50.410 --> 01:38:52.090
of fermentation, but also very salty.

01:38:52.090 --> 01:38:54.240
So there's a lot of effects that can....

01:38:54.240 --> 01:38:55.824
Yeah.
- Yeah, don't do what I do,

01:38:55.824 --> 01:38:58.303
- Yeah.
- At least not at the outset.

01:38:59.421 --> 01:39:02.524
So that is an experienced warning.

01:39:02.524 --> 01:39:04.370
So they did this, as I recall,

01:39:04.370 --> 01:39:06.970
there was a swap condition or
there was a halt condition.

01:39:06.970 --> 01:39:08.740
So you did controls, right?

01:39:08.740 --> 01:39:10.100
It wasn't just comparing groups.

01:39:10.100 --> 01:39:13.070
You had individuals in who
were initially in one group

01:39:13.070 --> 01:39:16.453
or the other moved to a
different group, correct?

01:39:17.460 --> 01:39:18.680
Or to stop and then return.

01:39:18.680 --> 01:39:20.620
- Yeah, we actually just did a stop

01:39:20.620 --> 01:39:22.267
and followed them during a washout phase.

01:39:22.267 --> 01:39:26.950
And the ideal situation for
dietary interventions like this

01:39:26.950 --> 01:39:29.672
are to do crossover studies,
as you're suggesting.

01:39:29.672 --> 01:39:32.570
We've recently completed a ketogenic

01:39:32.570 --> 01:39:34.430
versus Mediterranean diet intervention-

01:39:34.430 --> 01:39:35.890
- Are those data published yet?

01:39:35.890 --> 01:39:37.400
- Not yet, but Christopher's been Tweeting

01:39:37.400 --> 01:39:38.350
a lot of these data and

01:39:38.350 --> 01:39:39.790
there's a paper in revision right now.

01:39:39.790 --> 01:39:43.220
So if you go to Christopher
Gardner's Twitter feed,

01:39:43.220 --> 01:39:45.780
you'll be able to find him reporting

01:39:45.780 --> 01:39:48.147
some of the early results of this study.

01:39:48.147 --> 01:39:50.610
- Can you give us a snippet
of, was there a superior...

01:39:50.610 --> 01:39:52.680
Just give us a, you don't
have to tell us which one,

01:39:52.680 --> 01:39:55.800
but was there a superior condition

01:39:55.800 --> 01:39:57.790
of either Mediterranean versus ketogenic?

01:39:57.790 --> 01:40:02.500
- So the metabolic effects of
these, it's a beautiful study.

01:40:02.500 --> 01:40:04.420
I should let his group comment on that.

01:40:04.420 --> 01:40:07.290
The microbiota data we actually
are just generating now.

01:40:07.290 --> 01:40:10.960
So the study that his group
has put together from this

01:40:10.960 --> 01:40:13.737
is largely independent
of the microbiota data.

01:40:13.737 --> 01:40:14.990
- Right.
- And now we're doing

01:40:14.990 --> 01:40:17.010
a more in depth analysis,
and I'll have more to say

01:40:17.010 --> 01:40:17.990
about that the future.

01:40:17.990 --> 01:40:19.086
- We'll return to that, yeah.

01:40:19.086 --> 01:40:20.700
- But it's a super exciting study,

01:40:20.700 --> 01:40:23.958
because it is one of these
where people eat a certain way.

01:40:23.958 --> 01:40:26.220
And what's really beautiful about this

01:40:26.220 --> 01:40:29.670
is we even got food delivered
for part of the intervention.

01:40:29.670 --> 01:40:31.470
So we had complete control

01:40:31.470 --> 01:40:34.430
over what they at least
had available to eat.

01:40:34.430 --> 01:40:35.750
And then the second phase,

01:40:35.750 --> 01:40:40.550
they make the food on their own.

01:40:40.550 --> 01:40:43.229
And then we cross over
and do the same thing.

01:40:43.229 --> 01:40:45.187
And so that's really like,

01:40:45.187 --> 01:40:47.080
if you have a good enough budget,

01:40:47.080 --> 01:40:48.260
the right way to do a study like this.

01:40:48.260 --> 01:40:51.720
For this, we didn't have the time or money

01:40:51.720 --> 01:40:54.450
to do a crossover, but
we did do a washout phase

01:40:54.450 --> 01:40:57.590
where people, we didn't make
them stop eating, whatever,

01:40:57.590 --> 01:40:59.340
if they were enjoying
it, but we monitored.

01:40:59.340 --> 01:41:00.560
And there was some recidivism

01:41:00.560 --> 01:41:03.100
where there was a decrease
in fiber fermented food.

01:41:03.100 --> 01:41:04.810
And we could see, for instance,

01:41:04.810 --> 01:41:06.850
diversity start to plateau and reverse

01:41:06.850 --> 01:41:08.090
in many of these people.

01:41:08.090 --> 01:41:11.410
So there does appear to
be a need for maintenance

01:41:11.410 --> 01:41:13.980
of the intervention to maintain

01:41:13.980 --> 01:41:16.832
the perceived health benefits
that we were measuring.

01:41:16.832 --> 01:41:17.711
- Great.

01:41:17.711 --> 01:41:20.230
We will provide a link to
the study in the caption,

01:41:20.230 --> 01:41:23.389
and thank you for that very
clear and thorough description,

01:41:23.389 --> 01:41:27.610
from one of the investigators
involved in the study.

01:41:27.610 --> 01:41:30.304
It's great to go direct to the source.

01:41:30.304 --> 01:41:34.750
Anecdotally, were there movements in mood,

01:41:34.750 --> 01:41:38.420
in resistance to colds and infection

01:41:38.420 --> 01:41:39.508
during the course of the study?

01:41:39.508 --> 01:41:43.150
And this is kind of a prelude
to where I'm headed next,

01:41:43.150 --> 01:41:45.280
which is there is a
tremendous amount of interest

01:41:45.280 --> 01:41:46.640
in the so-called gut-brain axis.

01:41:46.640 --> 01:41:48.872
But also I want to make
sure that we talk about

01:41:48.872 --> 01:41:52.260
how these microbes and the
conditions they're establishing

01:41:52.260 --> 01:41:55.880
in the gut are creating positive
or negative health effects.

01:41:55.880 --> 01:41:58.270
I mean, basically how
signals get out of the gut.

01:41:58.270 --> 01:41:59.130
- Totally. Yeah.

01:41:59.130 --> 01:42:01.610
- So I certainly notice
that when I'm eating

01:42:01.610 --> 01:42:03.670
more fermented foods,
or there's probiotics

01:42:03.670 --> 01:42:05.530
in drinks I consume and so forth,

01:42:05.530 --> 01:42:08.010
that I feel quote, unquote, air quotes,

01:42:08.010 --> 01:42:09.820
completely subjective, I feel better.

01:42:09.820 --> 01:42:10.872
- Yeah.

01:42:10.872 --> 01:42:14.770
- I wish there was an objective
measure of feeling better.

01:42:14.770 --> 01:42:19.020
But I seem to think more
clearly, sleep better,

01:42:19.020 --> 01:42:21.180
mood, et cetera, and I
know I'm not alone in that.

01:42:21.180 --> 01:42:25.810
And any time I've taken harsh
antibiotics, I feel worse,

01:42:25.810 --> 01:42:27.070
but then again, I'm usually taking them

01:42:27.070 --> 01:42:29.040
because I'm feeling bad
about something else, right?

01:42:29.040 --> 01:42:30.690
I don't take them just because.

01:42:30.690 --> 01:42:33.550
So did people say they were
feeling better in any way?

01:42:33.550 --> 01:42:34.950
And if so, what did you have observe?

01:42:34.950 --> 01:42:37.598
And again, we're highlighting
these as anecdata.

01:42:37.598 --> 01:42:38.680
- Yeah, totally.

01:42:38.680 --> 01:42:43.680
We, as part of this effort to look at

01:42:44.280 --> 01:42:48.740
how dietary interventions
affect our health and wellbeing,

01:42:48.740 --> 01:42:51.560
and so forth, and
microbiome immune system,

01:42:51.560 --> 01:42:55.840
we interact with a lot of
people who have read our book,

01:42:55.840 --> 01:42:59.620
or kind of have become
microbiome enthusiasts.

01:42:59.620 --> 01:43:01.880
And have implemented
a lot of these changes

01:43:01.880 --> 01:43:02.713
in their personal life.

01:43:02.713 --> 01:43:04.870
And I hear the same thing
that you're saying, Andrew,

01:43:04.870 --> 01:43:07.660
that tons of people say
they have more energy.

01:43:07.660 --> 01:43:09.770
They think more clearly,
they sleep better,

01:43:09.770 --> 01:43:11.850
their family is nicer to each other,

01:43:11.850 --> 01:43:13.336
like the number of crazy things.

01:43:13.336 --> 01:43:15.560
And it's really hard to uncouple, like,

01:43:15.560 --> 01:43:18.570
is this because these
people have taken charge now

01:43:18.570 --> 01:43:20.450
of what they're eating and
just feel better in general

01:43:20.450 --> 01:43:23.230
for being in control
of what they're doing,

01:43:23.230 --> 01:43:26.690
or is there this cascading set of effects

01:43:26.690 --> 01:43:29.646
that are actually impacting our...

01:43:29.646 --> 01:43:31.906
Kind of emanating from the gut-brain axis.

01:43:31.906 --> 01:43:36.264
And so we actually implemented
a bunch of questionnaires

01:43:36.264 --> 01:43:40.540
and even a cognitive test to
try to get at some of this.

01:43:40.540 --> 01:43:44.040
And I should say, the list
of this goes on and on.

01:43:44.040 --> 01:43:46.290
There are people who claim
that their complexion improves,

01:43:46.290 --> 01:43:48.100
and that their allergies...

01:43:48.100 --> 01:43:50.280
And there's probably all
sorts of ripple effects.

01:43:50.280 --> 01:43:52.467
If you can affect your inflammation,

01:43:52.467 --> 01:43:54.700
we know that you can
affect your cognition,

01:43:54.700 --> 01:43:57.970
we know that you can affect your skin

01:43:57.970 --> 01:44:00.178
and inflammation that's
occurring on your skin.

01:44:00.178 --> 01:44:02.810
So I really think that there is a basis

01:44:02.810 --> 01:44:04.620
for a lot of those anecdotes.

01:44:04.620 --> 01:44:07.482
It may just be hard to
see in a short study

01:44:07.482 --> 01:44:12.482
and in a small cohort of people
over a short period of time,

01:44:13.120 --> 01:44:17.400
but we didn't really
see significant things

01:44:17.400 --> 01:44:21.466
associated with cognition and
moods and all of the things

01:44:21.466 --> 01:44:25.350
that we were testing for which, yeah,

01:44:25.350 --> 01:44:27.393
there could be a variety
of explanations for that.

01:44:27.393 --> 01:44:32.393
We also have a standardized
stool measure that people use,

01:44:34.240 --> 01:44:38.150
and there was less constipation,

01:44:38.150 --> 01:44:40.170
better bowel movements over the course

01:44:40.170 --> 01:44:41.310
of both of these interventions.

01:44:41.310 --> 01:44:44.240
So it did seem like bowel habits improved,

01:44:44.240 --> 01:44:46.770
which a lot of times can
lead to better moods,

01:44:46.770 --> 01:44:48.750
but that we weren't able to measure.

01:44:48.750 --> 01:44:50.600
- That the classic psychoanalyst

01:44:50.600 --> 01:44:53.260
would have a few field day with that.

01:44:53.260 --> 01:44:55.485
What sorts of interesting
things did you observe

01:44:55.485 --> 01:44:56.560
in the fiber group?

01:44:56.560 --> 01:44:59.030
Because it's clear that that group yielded

01:44:59.030 --> 01:45:02.520
some unexpected findings
in both directions.

01:45:02.520 --> 01:45:04.236
Things you expected to see, you didn't see

01:45:04.236 --> 01:45:06.150
to the same amplitude as you did

01:45:06.150 --> 01:45:07.280
in the fermented food group,

01:45:07.280 --> 01:45:09.810
but I'm guessing you also saw
some very interesting things

01:45:09.810 --> 01:45:10.910
in the fiber group.

01:45:10.910 --> 01:45:11.894
- Totally, yeah.

01:45:11.894 --> 01:45:14.850
So we started looking at
the data in more detail

01:45:14.850 --> 01:45:17.850
when we didn't see the
cohort-wide response.

01:45:17.850 --> 01:45:20.870
And one of the things we
observed is that in measuring

01:45:20.870 --> 01:45:22.040
all these immune parameters,

01:45:22.040 --> 01:45:23.853
there appeared to be
three different groups

01:45:23.853 --> 01:45:27.683
of immune responses that we were seeing.

01:45:27.683 --> 01:45:31.260
One group that got
overall less inflammatory,

01:45:31.260 --> 01:45:34.620
and then two other groups that
kind of had a mixed result,

01:45:34.620 --> 01:45:37.130
partly more inflammatory,
partly less inflammatory

01:45:37.130 --> 01:45:39.140
in all these markers
that we were looking at.

01:45:39.140 --> 01:45:43.370
And when we started digging
into like, what aspect

01:45:43.370 --> 01:45:47.260
of the biology of those people

01:45:47.260 --> 01:45:50.940
dictated or predicted
which group they fell into,

01:45:50.940 --> 01:45:52.670
the really interesting part is the people

01:45:52.670 --> 01:45:56.260
with highest diversity gut
microbiomes to start the study

01:45:56.260 --> 01:45:59.694
were the ones that were
most likely to have

01:45:59.694 --> 01:46:03.520
the decreases in inflammation.

01:46:03.520 --> 01:46:08.520
And so data seemed to be
telling us that if you start off

01:46:09.190 --> 01:46:11.767
with a diverse microbiota,
maybe one that's better equipped

01:46:11.767 --> 01:46:16.670
to degrade a wide
variety of dietary fiber,

01:46:16.670 --> 01:46:18.940
you're more likely to
respond positively to it.

01:46:18.940 --> 01:46:21.430
If you have a very
depleted gut microbiome,

01:46:21.430 --> 01:46:23.440
you're not as likely to
be able to respond to it.

01:46:23.440 --> 01:46:25.570
And thinking back to that
experiment that we talked about

01:46:25.570 --> 01:46:28.050
before with the multi-generational loss

01:46:28.050 --> 01:46:30.398
of fiber-fermenting microbes in mice

01:46:30.398 --> 01:46:34.310
that were fed a Western diet,

01:46:34.310 --> 01:46:38.370
it may be that many of us
in the industrialized world

01:46:38.370 --> 01:46:40.990
have a microbiome that's so depleted now,

01:46:40.990 --> 01:46:43.950
that even if we consume a high fiber diet,

01:46:43.950 --> 01:46:45.600
at least for a short period of time,

01:46:45.600 --> 01:46:47.580
we don't have the right
microbes in our gut

01:46:47.580 --> 01:46:48.413
to degrade that fiber.

01:46:48.413 --> 01:46:50.750
And this has actually been
observed by other groups,

01:46:50.750 --> 01:46:53.070
beautiful study out of
University of Minnesota,

01:46:53.070 --> 01:46:55.485
looking at immigrants,
coming to the United States.

01:46:55.485 --> 01:46:57.900
And within nine months,

01:46:57.900 --> 01:46:59.950
but certainly over the course of years,

01:46:59.950 --> 01:47:01.900
immigrants that come here lose

01:47:01.900 --> 01:47:05.297
a lot of the diversity
in their gut microbiome,

01:47:05.297 --> 01:47:08.010
but a lot of the fiber-degrading capacity

01:47:08.010 --> 01:47:09.250
in their gut microbiome too.

01:47:09.250 --> 01:47:11.030
So it could be that over time,

01:47:11.030 --> 01:47:12.930
this becomes a one way street,

01:47:12.930 --> 01:47:16.116
and it's hard for us to recover microbes

01:47:16.116 --> 01:47:18.020
that actually can degrade the fiber.

01:47:18.020 --> 01:47:20.040
And I think that this probably intersects

01:47:20.040 --> 01:47:21.547
with sanitation in our environment,

01:47:21.547 --> 01:47:23.393
and the fact that we don't have access

01:47:23.393 --> 01:47:27.200
to new microbes that might
help us degrade the fiber.

01:47:27.200 --> 01:47:30.237
That we actually have lost these microbes,

01:47:30.237 --> 01:47:32.760
and they're in some ways irrecoverable

01:47:32.760 --> 01:47:34.450
without deliberate reintroduction

01:47:34.450 --> 01:47:35.840
of fiber-degrading microbes.

01:47:35.840 --> 01:47:36.960
- I can recall from childhood,

01:47:36.960 --> 01:47:38.900
there were kids that would eat dirt.

01:47:38.900 --> 01:47:40.557
- Yeah.
- And snails and stuff.

01:47:40.557 --> 01:47:42.550
That just sounds totally disgusting,

01:47:42.550 --> 01:47:47.246
bu kids covered with mud
maybe not so much anymore.

01:47:47.246 --> 01:47:49.250
And certainly during the pandemic,

01:47:49.250 --> 01:47:52.130
there's been and a lot more
use of these hand sanitizers,

01:47:52.130 --> 01:47:54.380
that prior to that, people
seemed pretty spooked about,

01:47:54.380 --> 01:47:56.803
but then obviously they prioritized them.

01:47:59.520 --> 01:48:00.410
Well, you have children.

01:48:00.410 --> 01:48:02.890
Do you encourage them to...

01:48:02.890 --> 01:48:05.750
When they were young, did you
encourage them to interact

01:48:05.750 --> 01:48:07.396
with pets and dirt and-

01:48:07.396 --> 01:48:09.970
- Absolutely, yeah.
- Stuff in the environment,

01:48:09.970 --> 01:48:12.060
provided that stuff
wasn't immediately toxic?

01:48:12.060 --> 01:48:13.780
- Exactly. So this is really...

01:48:13.780 --> 01:48:17.983
It's a continual cost-benefit
analysis, I think.

01:48:18.840 --> 01:48:22.250
I will say that with the pandemic now,

01:48:22.250 --> 01:48:25.730
and certainly just with
infectious diseases in general,

01:48:25.730 --> 01:48:30.660
it's really important to
be aware of the possibility

01:48:30.660 --> 01:48:33.270
for compromising your health
through the spread of germs.

01:48:33.270 --> 01:48:36.050
And so that is just, hand
washing is important,

01:48:36.050 --> 01:48:39.773
and we have to be careful
with the spread of germs.

01:48:39.773 --> 01:48:44.773
But I do think that the
sanitization of our environment

01:48:46.807 --> 01:48:51.310
has gone overboard with various things

01:48:51.310 --> 01:48:53.507
being impregnated with antibiotics,

01:48:53.507 --> 01:48:57.208
shopping carts and things
like that, and toothbrushes.

01:48:57.208 --> 01:48:59.078
It's like antibiotics

01:48:59.078 --> 01:49:03.960
and things for killing
microbes are everywhere.

01:49:03.960 --> 01:49:07.120
And when we were raising...

01:49:07.120 --> 01:49:08.110
When our daughters were young

01:49:08.110 --> 01:49:10.280
and we were making these decisions,

01:49:10.280 --> 01:49:14.113
the calculations that we
would make were really one,

01:49:15.320 --> 01:49:19.050
how likely are they to encounter
a disease-causing microbe?

01:49:19.050 --> 01:49:22.062
If we've been out on a
hike or in our garden,

01:49:22.062 --> 01:49:24.860
just kind of working in
the dirt or whatever,

01:49:24.860 --> 01:49:26.880
maybe it's not as important
to wash your hands

01:49:26.880 --> 01:49:27.800
before you have lunch,

01:49:27.800 --> 01:49:29.880
even if there's a little
bit of dirt on them.

01:49:29.880 --> 01:49:31.490
If they've been in a public playground

01:49:31.490 --> 01:49:34.700
where maybe other kids with germs,

01:49:34.700 --> 01:49:37.230
or maybe even chemicals like pesticides

01:49:37.230 --> 01:49:39.646
and herbicides that are being used,

01:49:39.646 --> 01:49:42.414
maybe it's more important
than to wash your hands.

01:49:42.414 --> 01:49:44.660
Certainly if you've been
in the grocery store

01:49:44.660 --> 01:49:47.840
or on the subway, probably a
good idea to wash your hands.

01:49:47.840 --> 01:49:49.160
So I think you really need to think

01:49:49.160 --> 01:49:52.650
about kind of the context of it.

01:49:52.650 --> 01:49:56.210
Exposure to microbes from the environment

01:49:56.210 --> 01:50:00.130
is likely an important part of
educating our immune system,

01:50:00.130 --> 01:50:01.390
and keeping the proper balance

01:50:01.390 --> 01:50:02.223
in our immune system.

01:50:02.223 --> 01:50:03.571
And it's just a matter of figuring out

01:50:03.571 --> 01:50:05.620
the right way to do that safely.

01:50:05.620 --> 01:50:08.190
And it may be the fermented
food result that we saw

01:50:08.190 --> 01:50:10.420
is a way of tapping into
those same pathways,

01:50:10.420 --> 01:50:13.380
kind of an environmental
exposure to microbes that's safe.

01:50:13.380 --> 01:50:14.720
- Interesting.

01:50:14.720 --> 01:50:17.100
I'd like to touch on how signals get

01:50:17.100 --> 01:50:19.030
from the gut to the rest of the body.

01:50:19.030 --> 01:50:21.610
And we probably don't have time to go

01:50:21.610 --> 01:50:23.260
into all the systems that benefit

01:50:23.260 --> 01:50:26.350
from having a diverse microbiome
or healthy microbiome.

01:50:26.350 --> 01:50:29.655
But we talked about the immune system.

01:50:29.655 --> 01:50:32.990
There's active signaling
and transport from the gut,

01:50:32.990 --> 01:50:34.660
all along its length, as far as I know,

01:50:34.660 --> 01:50:37.080
into the bloodstream, into
other organs and tissues.

01:50:37.080 --> 01:50:41.720
So for the immune system,
it seems straightforward.

01:50:41.720 --> 01:50:43.850
It could reduce the amount or number

01:50:43.850 --> 01:50:46.560
of inflammatory cytokines,
like IL-6 and so forth,

01:50:46.560 --> 01:50:49.130
maybe increase the
anti-inflammatory cytokines,

01:50:49.130 --> 01:50:51.670
like IL-10 and others.

01:50:51.670 --> 01:50:54.130
But we know there's a
gut-brain access of neurons

01:50:54.130 --> 01:50:55.890
that literally talk in both directions

01:50:55.890 --> 01:50:57.200
between brain and gut.

01:50:57.200 --> 01:50:59.900
But let's say I'm eating
my fermented foods,

01:50:59.900 --> 01:51:01.190
I'm doing all the right things,

01:51:01.190 --> 01:51:04.760
and my gut is diverse and
I have all the goodies

01:51:04.760 --> 01:51:06.470
at all the right places.

01:51:06.470 --> 01:51:11.470
How is it that fact that
those microbiota are thriving

01:51:11.860 --> 01:51:14.513
is conveyed to the rest of the body?

01:51:14.513 --> 01:51:16.280
Because they're in
there doing their thing,

01:51:16.280 --> 01:51:18.010
and I don't know that they have a mind,

01:51:18.010 --> 01:51:18.980
but they're probably not thinking

01:51:18.980 --> 01:51:20.730
of taking care of me, Andrew.

01:51:20.730 --> 01:51:25.080
But I feel better, or I
might get sick less often,

01:51:25.080 --> 01:51:27.930
or combat any illness more quickly.

01:51:27.930 --> 01:51:30.110
How is that actually happening?

01:51:30.110 --> 01:51:33.419
Is it that the microbiomes
stay restricted to the gut,

01:51:33.419 --> 01:51:36.533
but the signaling molecules
are all downstream,

01:51:37.470 --> 01:51:40.610
in a downstream way, are making
good or bad things happen?

01:51:40.610 --> 01:51:43.440
Or is there some sort
of direct recognition

01:51:43.440 --> 01:51:45.840
at the body level, or are
there cells in the body

01:51:45.840 --> 01:51:48.660
that are responding to, ah,
the gut microbiome is healthy,

01:51:48.660 --> 01:51:51.590
and therefore I can make
more of the good stuff,

01:51:51.590 --> 01:51:53.320
and less of the bad stuff, so to speak.

01:51:53.320 --> 01:51:54.583
- Yeah, great.

01:51:55.585 --> 01:51:57.470
You're right, it's super complex.

01:51:57.470 --> 01:51:59.480
There's a huge array of ways

01:51:59.480 --> 01:52:04.480
that our body perceives both
the microbes and the molecules

01:52:04.570 --> 01:52:05.940
that they produce in our gut,

01:52:05.940 --> 01:52:07.900
and the molecules they
produce are, of course,

01:52:07.900 --> 01:52:09.420
a product of what microbes are there,

01:52:09.420 --> 01:52:12.370
and then what they receive
as kind of metabolic inputs,

01:52:12.370 --> 01:52:14.360
what we're eating and what other microbes

01:52:14.360 --> 01:52:17.620
are present in the environment,
providing molecules to them.

01:52:17.620 --> 01:52:20.420
So, it's this complex matrix, but we...

01:52:22.030 --> 01:52:24.030
Probably the simplest place to start

01:52:24.030 --> 01:52:25.260
is just the immune system.

01:52:25.260 --> 01:52:28.320
We have an immune system that,

01:52:28.320 --> 01:52:30.490
the vast majority of
immune cells in our body

01:52:30.490 --> 01:52:31.750
are located in our gut,

01:52:31.750 --> 01:52:35.190
just because there's
such a dense population

01:52:35.190 --> 01:52:37.617
of microbes there that have...

01:52:37.617 --> 01:52:41.440
We consider them beneficial microbes,

01:52:41.440 --> 01:52:42.840
but they're only beneficial if they're

01:52:42.840 --> 01:52:44.080
in the right spot in the gut.

01:52:44.080 --> 01:52:45.800
As soon as they mislocalize,

01:52:45.800 --> 01:52:48.760
we know that they can become
opportunistic pathogens.

01:52:48.760 --> 01:52:51.860
And so the immune system really
playing an important role

01:52:51.860 --> 01:52:56.300
to keep with them in place
is essential for this system

01:52:56.300 --> 01:52:58.970
not moving into a disease space.

01:52:58.970 --> 01:53:01.170
The immune system has a variety of ways

01:53:01.170 --> 01:53:02.733
of monitoring what microbes are there.

01:53:02.733 --> 01:53:05.867
They're actually specialized
to structures in the gut

01:53:05.867 --> 01:53:10.860
known as peyer's patches that
actually take up microbes.

01:53:10.860 --> 01:53:14.370
They actually allow microbes to transit

01:53:14.370 --> 01:53:18.299
into this population of immune cells

01:53:18.299 --> 01:53:20.770
in a very controlled way,

01:53:20.770 --> 01:53:23.730
so that that set of immune
cells becomes educated

01:53:23.730 --> 01:53:25.410
as to what microbes are just

01:53:25.410 --> 01:53:26.800
on the other side of the barrier.

01:53:26.800 --> 01:53:28.900
- Wow, kind of like a border patrol.

01:53:28.900 --> 01:53:29.870
- Exactly, yeah.

01:53:29.870 --> 01:53:33.068
So they bring them in,
they fingerprint them,

01:53:33.068 --> 01:53:38.068
and then have kind of
this set of responses

01:53:39.430 --> 01:53:41.360
ready to go if needed.

01:53:41.360 --> 01:53:42.390
- [Andrew Huberman] Amazing.

01:53:42.390 --> 01:53:44.970
- There are other cells
known as dendritic cells,

01:53:44.970 --> 01:53:48.750
special type types that
actually send long arms,

01:53:48.750 --> 01:53:51.630
these processes out into
the lumen of the gut,

01:53:51.630 --> 01:53:53.960
and do the same thing, take up microbes,

01:53:53.960 --> 01:53:55.704
bring them back in and sample them.

01:53:55.704 --> 01:53:59.150
In addition to these
direct sampling mechanisms,

01:53:59.150 --> 01:54:03.050
the cells that line the gut
have a huge array of receptors,

01:54:03.050 --> 01:54:06.760
specialized proteins,
that perceive patterns,

01:54:06.760 --> 01:54:10.120
molecular patterns that the microbes make.

01:54:10.120 --> 01:54:13.610
So things like endotoxin
lipopolysaccharide,

01:54:13.610 --> 01:54:16.720
just the cell wall of the bacteria.

01:54:16.720 --> 01:54:19.580
We have specialized receptors
that recognize those.

01:54:19.580 --> 01:54:21.760
If those signals become too profound,

01:54:21.760 --> 01:54:24.670
or if they're perceived
in the wrong place,

01:54:24.670 --> 01:54:26.760
that can stimulate an
inflammatory response.

01:54:26.760 --> 01:54:29.880
So there's all these ways of
monitoring the membership,

01:54:29.880 --> 01:54:32.080
and where it is and how close it is.

01:54:32.080 --> 01:54:35.760
But then there's this
whole other set of ways

01:54:35.760 --> 01:54:37.930
of perceiving metabolic activity,

01:54:37.930 --> 01:54:39.000
and what's happening in the gut.

01:54:39.000 --> 01:54:40.313
And you mentioned before,

01:54:41.474 --> 01:54:46.210
these cell types, that express
taste receptors in the gut

01:54:46.210 --> 01:54:48.980
and have ways of sampling
dietary components.

01:54:48.980 --> 01:54:53.980
They're the same types of,
or analogous cells in our gut

01:54:54.745 --> 01:54:58.620
that are perceiving metabolites
produced by the microbiota

01:54:58.620 --> 01:55:00.610
so that our bodies can perceive

01:55:00.610 --> 01:55:03.380
what sort of metabolic
activity is going on.

01:55:03.380 --> 01:55:05.780
And then in addition to that,

01:55:05.780 --> 01:55:10.610
there's this tremendously
important nervous system

01:55:10.610 --> 01:55:13.220
that's sending signals back to the brain,

01:55:13.220 --> 01:55:15.670
dictating things like motility.

01:55:15.670 --> 01:55:18.490
Do I get rid of what's in here?
Do I move it along quickly?

01:55:18.490 --> 01:55:20.690
What actually is happening?

01:55:20.690 --> 01:55:23.210
Do I need to interact with immune cells?

01:55:23.210 --> 01:55:24.990
So there's this really complex array

01:55:24.990 --> 01:55:28.470
of interactions between
the different cell types.

01:55:28.470 --> 01:55:31.780
And then a lot of the
cells that are in the gut,

01:55:31.780 --> 01:55:33.330
perceiving all of these signals,

01:55:33.330 --> 01:55:37.130
a lot of the immune cells can
actually get up and leave.

01:55:37.130 --> 01:55:39.550
They can get into the
blood, cycle through,

01:55:39.550 --> 01:55:44.360
and then home to other regions
of the mucosal surfaces,

01:55:44.360 --> 01:55:48.470
so that mucosal surfaces
are educated broadly

01:55:48.470 --> 01:55:51.270
against what's passing through our gut.

01:55:51.270 --> 01:55:55.860
So there's a variety of
ways of cells communicating,

01:55:55.860 --> 01:56:00.620
and then a lot of the molecules
that the microbiota makes

01:56:00.620 --> 01:56:04.100
can actually make their way
into the bloodstream directly.

01:56:04.100 --> 01:56:08.610
And so the array of molecules
is still being defined,

01:56:08.610 --> 01:56:11.530
we're trying to figure out
what all these chemicals are.

01:56:11.530 --> 01:56:13.290
We've mentioned the
short-chain fatty acids,

01:56:13.290 --> 01:56:15.370
but those are just the tip of the iceberg.

01:56:15.370 --> 01:56:17.230
They are really interesting compounds

01:56:17.230 --> 01:56:21.350
like indole derivatives and phenols,

01:56:22.559 --> 01:56:26.480
derived from amino acids,
metabolized by gut microbes,

01:56:26.480 --> 01:56:28.200
taken up into the bloodstream.

01:56:28.200 --> 01:56:30.440
And then we further metabolize these,

01:56:30.440 --> 01:56:33.930
they become kind of
co-microbe host metabolites,

01:56:33.930 --> 01:56:35.770
and then they can go on and bind

01:56:35.770 --> 01:56:38.110
to different receptors
throughout our body,

01:56:38.110 --> 01:56:40.360
anywhere our bloodstream has access to,

01:56:40.360 --> 01:56:43.360
and start to trigger signaling cascades.

01:56:43.360 --> 01:56:45.420
- Is it known whether or
not any of those molecules

01:56:45.420 --> 01:56:47.970
are small enough to cross
the blood-brain barrier?

01:56:47.970 --> 01:56:51.720
Because the hypothesis it
and the current thinking

01:56:51.720 --> 01:56:55.540
is that neurotransmitters
manufactured in the gut,

01:56:55.540 --> 01:56:57.710
and signaling along the gut-brain axis,

01:56:57.710 --> 01:57:00.580
literally neurons talking
back and forth electrically

01:57:00.580 --> 01:57:02.470
from brain to gut and gut to brain

01:57:02.470 --> 01:57:03.777
is what regulates things like mood,

01:57:03.777 --> 01:57:05.370
or at least in animal models.

01:57:05.370 --> 01:57:07.380
And there are some emerging human studies,

01:57:07.380 --> 01:57:11.410
improvement of symptoms in
autism spectrum disorders,

01:57:11.410 --> 01:57:12.820
maybe even in ADHD.

01:57:12.820 --> 01:57:13.730
What I'm basically saying here

01:57:13.730 --> 01:57:16.480
is there is some evidence emerging

01:57:16.480 --> 01:57:18.140
that improving the gut microbiome

01:57:18.140 --> 01:57:20.620
can improve outcomes in psychiatric

01:57:20.620 --> 01:57:22.330
and developmental disorders.

01:57:22.330 --> 01:57:26.150
But what you're telling me is
that the microbiota themselves

01:57:26.150 --> 01:57:27.440
are manufacturing chemicals

01:57:27.440 --> 01:57:28.760
that can make into the bloodstream.

01:57:28.760 --> 01:57:30.770
And therefore I'm asking
if those chemicals

01:57:30.770 --> 01:57:33.390
can move from the bloodstream
into the brain directly.

01:57:33.390 --> 01:57:37.370
It may not be a gut-brain
axis via neurons.

01:57:37.370 --> 01:57:38.750
It actually could just be seepage

01:57:38.750 --> 01:57:40.527
of serotonin into the brain,

01:57:40.527 --> 01:57:43.700
or acetylcholine into the
brain for that matter.

01:57:43.700 --> 01:57:44.533
- Totally, yeah.

01:57:47.383 --> 01:57:51.130
The biology of most of these molecules

01:57:51.130 --> 01:57:52.070
is not well understood,

01:57:52.070 --> 01:57:54.430
but certainly in like
cerebral spinal fluid

01:57:54.430 --> 01:57:55.683
that's been analyzed,

01:57:56.920 --> 01:57:59.540
you can perceive these
microbial metabolites,

01:57:59.540 --> 01:58:01.076
so they are there.
- That's the answer, yeah.

01:58:01.076 --> 01:58:03.400
Some of them are getting
across the barrier.

01:58:03.400 --> 01:58:05.080
- But so a really interesting thing is,

01:58:05.080 --> 01:58:08.426
I think a lot of these molecules are,

01:58:08.426 --> 01:58:11.380
if they're experienced
at high enough doses,

01:58:11.380 --> 01:58:14.360
are toxic or have toxic properties.

01:58:14.360 --> 01:58:17.630
We know that a lot of these metabolites,

01:58:17.630 --> 01:58:18.925
when they make their way
into the bloodstream,

01:58:18.925 --> 01:58:21.760
eventually are excreted
through the kidneys in urine.

01:58:21.760 --> 01:58:23.860
So actually we can monitor the metabolism

01:58:23.860 --> 01:58:26.260
that's going on in your
gut by actually looking

01:58:26.260 --> 01:58:28.220
at the metabolites that
are present in your urine,

01:58:28.220 --> 01:58:31.690
because many of those
originated in your gut,

01:58:31.690 --> 01:58:33.060
from your gut microbes.

01:58:33.060 --> 01:58:35.001
But people with kidney disease,

01:58:35.001 --> 01:58:37.590
whose kidneys' filtering processes

01:58:37.590 --> 01:58:39.240
is not functioning properly,

01:58:39.240 --> 01:58:43.950
actually build up high levels
of many of these metabolites

01:58:43.950 --> 01:58:46.050
into the bloodstream,

01:58:46.050 --> 01:58:50.430
and that can lead to
more of these molecules

01:58:50.430 --> 01:58:52.760
making it across the blood-brain barrier.

01:58:52.760 --> 01:58:56.060
And in fact, some of the
transporters in the kidney

01:58:56.060 --> 01:58:58.410
that are responsible for
shuttling these molecules

01:58:58.410 --> 01:59:01.509
out into urine, are also
founded the blood-brain barrier

01:59:01.509 --> 01:59:04.560
for shuttling the molecules
back into the bloodstream

01:59:04.560 --> 01:59:06.240
if they do get across.
- Incredible.

01:59:06.240 --> 01:59:10.370
- And we know that mental
fog one of the big symptoms

01:59:10.370 --> 01:59:14.019
of kidney disease, potentially
because a lot of these

01:59:14.019 --> 01:59:16.270
metabolites accumulate in blood,

01:59:16.270 --> 01:59:19.140
and then make their way
across the blood-brain barrier

01:59:19.140 --> 01:59:21.400
into, yeah, the central nervous system.

01:59:21.400 --> 01:59:23.200
- Amazing. I'm glad you
mentioned mental fog.

01:59:23.200 --> 01:59:25.923
A few years back, there were some reports,

01:59:26.806 --> 01:59:30.100
some scientific reports and
as a consequence in the media,

01:59:30.100 --> 01:59:33.870
that excessive intake
of pill-form probiotics

01:59:33.870 --> 01:59:35.590
could create mental fog.

01:59:35.590 --> 01:59:37.990
I don't know if that ever took hold.

01:59:37.990 --> 01:59:42.060
And it raises a general question
about pill form probiotics.

01:59:42.060 --> 01:59:43.420
I took them for a few years,

01:59:43.420 --> 01:59:45.530
just thinking that would be
good for my gut microbiome,

01:59:45.530 --> 01:59:47.360
and then I switched to
the fermented food thing,

01:59:47.360 --> 01:59:50.100
largely there's a consequence of the work

01:59:50.100 --> 01:59:52.198
that you and Chris published.

01:59:52.198 --> 01:59:55.500
But what what's the
thought about probiotics

01:59:55.500 --> 01:59:57.280
for the typical person
that's not recovering

01:59:57.280 --> 01:59:58.602
from a round of antibiotics

01:59:58.602 --> 02:00:01.163
or that has been prescribed them?

02:00:02.064 --> 02:00:06.410
I've heard that the species of microbiota

02:00:06.410 --> 02:00:10.040
that they proliferate
might not be the species

02:00:10.040 --> 02:00:11.360
that we want to proliferate,

02:00:11.360 --> 02:00:13.430
but I've also heard that
maybe that doesn't matter.

02:00:13.430 --> 02:00:14.550
So what's your general stance?

02:00:14.550 --> 02:00:16.050
They can be quite expensive.

02:00:16.050 --> 02:00:16.883
- Yeah.
- Also,

02:00:16.883 --> 02:00:18.410
I know I've been talking
about expense a lot today,

02:00:18.410 --> 02:00:19.600
but I always want to take into account

02:00:19.600 --> 02:00:21.550
that people are showing up to the table

02:00:22.880 --> 02:00:25.350
with a variety of budgets,
and probiotics are one

02:00:25.350 --> 02:00:27.190
of the more expensive
supplements out there.

02:00:27.190 --> 02:00:29.460
You can quickly get into the
several hundreds of dollars

02:00:29.460 --> 02:00:32.560
per month if you're
getting the quote, unquote,

02:00:32.560 --> 02:00:34.176
best quality ones.
- Right.

02:00:34.176 --> 02:00:35.810
- And if they're actually
causing brain fog,

02:00:35.810 --> 02:00:37.860
then I'm not sure I'd want to use them.

02:00:37.860 --> 02:00:38.707
- No, completely.

02:00:38.707 --> 02:00:39.916
And there's a ton of snake oil out there.

02:00:39.916 --> 02:00:42.010
People know that they...

02:00:42.010 --> 02:00:45.840
I think many of these companies are aware

02:00:45.840 --> 02:00:47.500
that they can prey off of people's fears

02:00:47.500 --> 02:00:50.160
and get a lot of money from
them with absolutely no data

02:00:50.160 --> 02:00:52.660
to back up that their
probiotic is doing anything.

02:00:53.630 --> 02:00:56.700
So I think the first thing
to say is buyer beware,

02:00:56.700 --> 02:01:00.010
because it's a supplement
market, it's largely unregulated.

02:01:00.010 --> 02:01:01.100
And that means that there

02:01:01.100 --> 02:01:04.560
are a lot of bad products out
there, and a lot of products

02:01:04.560 --> 02:01:07.620
that even though they're
not intended to be bad,

02:01:07.620 --> 02:01:09.430
just don't have great quality control.

02:01:09.430 --> 02:01:11.730
There have been several
studies that have taken

02:01:11.730 --> 02:01:13.810
off the over the counter,

02:01:13.810 --> 02:01:15.720
just kind of off the shelf probiotics,

02:01:15.720 --> 02:01:18.990
surveyed what's in there
based on sequencing

02:01:18.990 --> 02:01:22.560
and shown what is in there

02:01:22.560 --> 02:01:24.680
does not match what's on the label.

02:01:24.680 --> 02:01:26.220
- And that's true of many supplements,

02:01:26.220 --> 02:01:27.815
and unfortunately supplement companies.

02:01:27.815 --> 02:01:29.540
This is something we get
into on the podcast a lot.

02:01:29.540 --> 02:01:32.782
There are reputable brands and
they go through a lot of work

02:01:32.782 --> 02:01:35.650
to get things right, and there are many

02:01:35.650 --> 02:01:37.880
that just for whatever reason,

02:01:37.880 --> 02:01:39.490
it just doesn't match what's listed.

02:01:39.490 --> 02:01:44.490
- Exactly, and so there are
places that probiotic companies

02:01:44.810 --> 02:01:48.280
can send their product to have
it independently validated.

02:01:48.280 --> 02:01:50.240
So you want to look for that sort

02:01:50.240 --> 02:01:52.104
of validation on a product.

02:01:52.104 --> 02:01:55.480
There also are names that
are just very well-known,

02:01:55.480 --> 02:01:59.087
and their reputations are on the line,

02:02:02.270 --> 02:02:05.120
so they probably invest a little
bit more in quality control

02:02:05.120 --> 02:02:08.560
than maybe some of the
other, lesser-known names.

02:02:08.560 --> 02:02:13.560
But there's a huge range
of data on probiotics.

02:02:14.260 --> 02:02:18.573
And I think, the thing that
we kind of recommend is,

02:02:19.433 --> 02:02:22.960
try to find good products and
then experiment for yourself

02:02:22.960 --> 02:02:24.810
and see if you can find
something that works for you.

02:02:24.810 --> 02:02:26.241
I know people who have experienced

02:02:26.241 --> 02:02:31.080
constipation and don't
want to change their diet,

02:02:31.080 --> 02:02:33.870
and have found a probiotic
that helps them with that.

02:02:33.870 --> 02:02:36.800
If you can find that right mix, great.

02:02:36.800 --> 02:02:38.470
That's wonderful.

02:02:38.470 --> 02:02:40.830
I would say that the data right now

02:02:40.830 --> 02:02:45.830
is not overwhelmingly positive
for what probiotics do

02:02:46.850 --> 02:02:48.610
to the gut microbiota.

02:02:48.610 --> 02:02:50.210
So there have been some
nice studies looking

02:02:50.210 --> 02:02:52.990
at the impact of probiotics on recovery

02:02:52.990 --> 02:02:55.020
after antibiotic treatment.

02:02:55.020 --> 02:02:56.880
And it appears to slow down the recovery

02:02:56.880 --> 02:02:58.770
of the mucosal microbiota.

02:02:58.770 --> 02:03:03.770
And some other studies that
have where the big signal

02:03:05.320 --> 02:03:07.480
isn't seen, as you might hope,

02:03:07.480 --> 02:03:09.050
with a probiotic that's supposed

02:03:09.050 --> 02:03:10.400
to treat a different disease.

02:03:10.400 --> 02:03:12.890
There have been
metaanalysis that do suggest

02:03:12.890 --> 02:03:17.690
in certain instances, recovery
from antibiotics that there,

02:03:17.690 --> 02:03:19.720
even though it may cause your microbiome

02:03:19.720 --> 02:03:20.830
to recover more slowly,

02:03:20.830 --> 02:03:24.160
that it may actually
prevent diarrheal disease,

02:03:24.160 --> 02:03:27.440
recovery from viral diarrheas,
probiotics may help.

02:03:27.440 --> 02:03:30.010
But because there's such
a huge range of products,

02:03:30.010 --> 02:03:33.070
and because each person
is their own little caper

02:03:33.070 --> 02:03:34.321
when it comes to the microbiome,

02:03:34.321 --> 02:03:39.321
it's really hard to know
whether there are great products

02:03:39.530 --> 02:03:41.100
for a given indication.

02:03:41.100 --> 02:03:44.150
The really good advice that
I've heard is try to find

02:03:45.310 --> 02:03:48.760
a study that supports in...

02:03:48.760 --> 02:03:51.130
A really well designed
study, and this is very hard

02:03:51.130 --> 02:03:53.550
for people who aren't
scientists to evaluate.

02:03:53.550 --> 02:03:56.190
But so if you're experiencing
a medical problem,

02:03:56.190 --> 02:04:00.540
or want to consult a doctor,
that that might be helpful.

02:04:00.540 --> 02:04:03.320
But finding a study where
a specific probiotic

02:04:03.320 --> 02:04:07.090
has successfully done whatever
it is you're looking for,

02:04:07.090 --> 02:04:09.131
and then sticking with that probiotic

02:04:09.131 --> 02:04:12.470
is really the best recipe
as a place to start

02:04:12.470 --> 02:04:13.303
in this space, I think.

02:04:13.303 --> 02:04:14.560
- And what about prebiotics?

02:04:14.560 --> 02:04:17.560
Is there are a number of
reasons why, I can imagine,

02:04:17.560 --> 02:04:20.270
that prebiotics would be beneficial?

02:04:20.270 --> 02:04:23.880
Which essentially it's, you're
pushing the fiber system,

02:04:23.880 --> 02:04:25.130
which we've talked a lot about today.

02:04:25.130 --> 02:04:26.453
- Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

02:04:29.100 --> 02:04:33.450
The studies that have
been done on prebiotics,

02:04:33.450 --> 02:04:36.683
it's really kind of a
mixed bag of results.

02:04:37.710 --> 02:04:41.170
There have been studies
done with purified fibers

02:04:41.170 --> 02:04:43.780
where you actually see
micro biodiversity plummet

02:04:43.780 --> 02:04:44.830
over the course of the study,

02:04:44.830 --> 02:04:47.820
because you get a very specific bloom

02:04:47.820 --> 02:04:49.820
in a small number of
bacteria that are good

02:04:49.820 --> 02:04:52.160
at using that one type of fiber.

02:04:52.160 --> 02:04:54.330
And that's at the expense
of all the other microbes

02:04:54.330 --> 02:04:55.310
that are in the gut.

02:04:55.310 --> 02:04:59.750
And so it's really hard to
replicate with purified fiber

02:04:59.750 --> 02:05:01.760
what you'd get, for
instance, at a salad bar,

02:05:01.760 --> 02:05:04.455
in terms of the array
of complex carbohydrates

02:05:04.455 --> 02:05:06.870
that you would be exposing
your microbiota to.

02:05:06.870 --> 02:05:08.410
And I think the kind of

02:05:11.870 --> 02:05:13.770
broad view of this in the field

02:05:13.770 --> 02:05:17.555
is that consuming a
broad variety of plants,

02:05:17.555 --> 02:05:21.580
and all the diverse fiber
that comes with that

02:05:21.580 --> 02:05:23.640
is probably better in fostering diversity

02:05:23.640 --> 02:05:26.210
in your microbiota than purified fibers.

02:05:26.210 --> 02:05:28.230
Now, there are, again,

02:05:28.230 --> 02:05:30.780
a lot of people who benefit
from purified fibers,

02:05:30.780 --> 02:05:35.700
either for GI motility or for
other aspects of GI health,

02:05:35.700 --> 02:05:37.460
problems that they've been experiencing.

02:05:37.460 --> 02:05:38.750
Again, I think it's the type of thing

02:05:38.750 --> 02:05:41.240
where you have to try to find the thing

02:05:41.240 --> 02:05:42.166
that's right for you.

02:05:42.166 --> 02:05:46.400
But there also are studies that suggest

02:05:46.400 --> 02:05:50.910
that if you layer rapidly
fermentable fibers

02:05:50.910 --> 02:05:55.470
on top of a Western diet,
you actually can result

02:05:55.470 --> 02:05:58.710
in weird metabolism
happening in your liver,

02:05:58.710 --> 02:06:01.700
because you have this incredibly
rapid fermentation of fiber

02:06:01.700 --> 02:06:04.750
along with a lot of fat
coming into the system.

02:06:04.750 --> 02:06:06.760
At least that's the theory.

02:06:06.760 --> 02:06:09.850
And in a mouse study that was
published a few years ago,

02:06:09.850 --> 02:06:11.860
they actually see that
a subset of the mice

02:06:11.860 --> 02:06:15.170
develop hepatocellular
carcinoma when they're fed

02:06:15.170 --> 02:06:17.466
a high dose prebiotic-
- Liver cancer.

02:06:17.466 --> 02:06:21.230
- Liver cancer, a on
top of a Western diet.

02:06:21.230 --> 02:06:23.960
So whether that's
representative of human biology,

02:06:23.960 --> 02:06:24.980
we don't know,

02:06:24.980 --> 02:06:28.638
but purified fibers are
definitely very different,

02:06:28.638 --> 02:06:30.970
both in terms of the
diversity of structures,

02:06:30.970 --> 02:06:33.580
but also in terms of how rapidly

02:06:33.580 --> 02:06:34.790
they're fermented in the gut.

02:06:34.790 --> 02:06:38.136
Because if you are eating plants,

02:06:38.136 --> 02:06:41.690
the complex structures there
really slow the microbes down

02:06:41.690 --> 02:06:44.370
in terms of fermentation, and
you end up with a slow rate

02:06:44.370 --> 02:06:46.900
of fermentation over the
length of your colon,

02:06:46.900 --> 02:06:50.060
as opposed to this big burst
of fermentation that can happen

02:06:50.060 --> 02:06:52.930
if you eat something
that is highly soluble

02:06:52.930 --> 02:06:56.040
and easily accessed by the microbes.

02:06:56.040 --> 02:06:57.260
- Interesting, so I guess,

02:06:57.260 --> 02:06:58.870
is it fair to come back to this idea?

02:06:58.870 --> 02:07:00.796
Try and avoid processed foods,

02:07:00.796 --> 02:07:03.280
the highly palatable foods,

02:07:03.280 --> 02:07:05.450
they're all sometimes super
highly palatable foods.

02:07:05.450 --> 02:07:06.510
They're now called,

02:07:06.510 --> 02:07:08.520
that are packed with
hidden sugars, emulsifiers.

02:07:08.520 --> 02:07:11.808
So it sounds like some fiber is good,

02:07:11.808 --> 02:07:14.010
and despite the outcome of the study,

02:07:14.010 --> 02:07:15.390
you identified that if you have

02:07:15.390 --> 02:07:17.600
the appropriate microbiota background,

02:07:18.473 --> 02:07:21.760
then one will even better to the fiber,

02:07:21.760 --> 02:07:24.620
maybe a longer ramp up
phase for those folks,

02:07:24.620 --> 02:07:26.550
and then the fermented foods.

02:07:26.550 --> 02:07:28.080
'Cause there's no reason
why you can't do both.

02:07:28.080 --> 02:07:29.710
And as we've talked about before,

02:07:29.710 --> 02:07:31.840
a lot of fermented foods have fiber.

02:07:31.840 --> 02:07:33.770
So you can kill two birds with one stone.

02:07:33.770 --> 02:07:34.603
- Totally.

02:07:34.603 --> 02:07:36.740
And it could be that the
diversity increase that we saw

02:07:36.740 --> 02:07:39.550
in the high fermented food
group could be something

02:07:39.550 --> 02:07:41.850
that would aid the high fiber group.

02:07:41.850 --> 02:07:43.936
And so now we're planning
another study coming up

02:07:43.936 --> 02:07:46.840
where we're doing high
fiber, high fermented food,

02:07:46.840 --> 02:07:48.520
and then fiber plus fermented food,

02:07:48.520 --> 02:07:49.947
just to see if there's a
synergistic effect there.

02:07:49.947 --> 02:07:53.110
- Great, I want to enroll, seriously.

02:07:53.110 --> 02:07:54.220
Although I guess I'm biased,

02:07:54.220 --> 02:07:56.096
'cause I was sort of know
where you're trying to..

02:07:56.096 --> 02:07:58.620
Well, is it blood draws that you use

02:07:58.620 --> 02:08:00.210
to measure the inflammatome?

02:08:00.210 --> 02:08:01.290
- Exactly, so we do blood draws-

02:08:01.290 --> 02:08:02.966
- [Andrew Huberman] Blood draws don't lie,

02:08:02.966 --> 02:08:03.960
so that's good.
- Yep, yep, right.

02:08:03.960 --> 02:08:06.900
- So you've covered a tremendous
amount of information,

02:08:06.900 --> 02:08:08.150
